const FOCUS_TOPICS = {
  "indexes": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Indexes",
    "body": "**An index is a separate structure, usually a B-tree, that lets the database find rows without reading the whole table**. You add one when a query filters, joins, or sorts on a column and the table is large enough that a scan shows up in the plan.\n\nA composite index on `(customer_id, created_at)` serves a filter on `customer_id` alone, and a filter on both, because of the leftmost prefix. It does not serve a filter on `created_at` alone. Put the equality column first and the range column last.\n\nEvery index speeds reads and slows writes. Each insert and update maintains every index on the table. A table with ten indexes is a write amplifier. Drop the ones no plan uses.\n\nThe failure mode is a query that looks indexed but isn't: a function wrapped around the column, a leading wildcard, or an implicit cast. The plan goes back to a sequential scan, and that scan is what falls over at peak.\n\n**Add the index when the plan shows a scan, and stop there.** *A write still maintains every index, so a column nobody filters on is pure cost.*\n\nOn an orders table the screen is \"this customer's orders, newest first.\" The index is `(customer_id, created_at DESC)`. The equality column is first, the range is last. A query that only has the date cannot use it, and you do not add a second index \"just in case\" until that query shows up in the plan. A covering index that includes the status column avoids the heap fetch for the list page. It gets wider, and every insert pays for that width.\n\nWhat you would not do is index every foreign key because a checklist said so. Ten indexes on a hot table turn a cheap insert into ten tree updates, and the write path is often the one that is already short of capacity."
  },
  "query-plans": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Query plans",
    "body": "**The planner chooses how to run a statement**. `EXPLAIN` shows the choice. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` runs it and shows actual rows and time. You read it when a query is slow, not as a habit on every statement.\n\nLook for a sequential scan on a big table, a nested loop that runs thousands of times, and a row estimate that is wildly different from the actual row count. Bad estimates usually mean stale statistics. `ANALYZE` fixes that. A missing index is the other common cause.\n\nYou pick an index or a rewrite over a bigger database when the plan shows the database is doing dumb work. You pick a bigger database or a replica when the plan is already a tight index lookup and the machine is simply out of CPU or IO.\n\nThe trap is optimizing the plan you ran on an empty staging table. Estimates change with data volume. Capture the plan from production, or from a copy of production data.\n\n**Read the plan from production-shaped data, then change the smallest thing that removes the dumb work.** *A bigger box is the wrong next step when the plan is a sequential scan.*\n\nA typical miss is a nested loop: the outer query returns 5,000 orders, and for each one the database looks up the customer with no index. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows the loop and a row estimate of 10 against an actual of 5,000. That gap is stale statistics or a predicate the planner cannot see, such as `WHERE lower(email) = ...`. You add an expression index, or you write the predicate so it matches the column, and you run `ANALYZE`.\n\nYou would not paste the plan from an empty staging database into the interview and call it done. Estimates follow the data. You also would not hint every query. A hint freezes a choice that was right for yesterday's volume."
  },
  "transactions-acid": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Transactions and ACID",
    "body": "**A transaction is the set of writes that succeed or fail together**. ACID is the promise around that set.\n\nAtomic: the charge and the order row commit together, or neither does. Consistent: constraints and invariants hold at commit. Isolated: concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-finished work, to the degree the isolation level allows. Durable: after commit returns, a crash does not lose the write. That durability is the write-ahead log, flushed to disk.\n\nYou put two writes in one transaction when they are in the same database and the caller needs both or neither. You do not stretch one transaction across a payment provider and your database. That hold time is how you lock rows and exhaust the pool.\n\nThe failure interviewers want: a commit that returned, then a crash. The data is still there because the log was flushed. A timeout before the response does not tell you whether it committed. Look it up. Don't repeat the write blindly.\n\n**One database, one transaction, for the rows that must commit together.** *A timeout is not a rollback you can see.*\n\nThe order row and the payment-attempt row live in the same Postgres. You insert both, and the commit returns only after the write-ahead log is flushed. That is the durability you are allowed to claim. The card network is a different system. You do not hold the order-row lock while that HTTP call runs. The pool fills with transactions that are \"open\" only because a provider is slow.\n\nIf the client times out, you look up the idempotency key before you insert again. A commit that reached disk and a response that never reached the client look the same from the outside. Repeating the insert is how you get two orders. A read replica is not where you check, because the replica may not have the commit yet."
  },
  "isolation-levels": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Isolation levels",
    "body": "**Isolation is how much concurrent transactions can interfere**. Databases differ. Postgres defaults to Read Committed. Many interviews assume you can name the anomalies.\n\nDirty read: you see another transaction's uncommitted write. Read Committed already prevents that. Non-repeatable read: you read a row twice and get two values because someone committed in between. Phantom: the same query returns a new row the second time.\n\nRepeatable Read, in Postgres, is implemented with MVCC: the transaction sees a snapshot. Writers don't block readers. A conflicting update fails with a serialization error instead of silently overwriting. Serializable is stricter and aborts more often.\n\nYou stay on Read Committed for most CRUD. You move up when a report or a money movement must not see a torn state, and you are ready to retry aborts.\n\nThe mistake is picking Serializable \"to be safe\" on a hot checkout path. Aborts become the outage. The other mistake is assuming the default prevents lost updates. It does not. You still need a version column or `SELECT FOR UPDATE`.\n\n**Stay on Read Committed until a real anomaly forces a change, and then retry the abort.** *Serializable does not make a hot row faster.*\n\nRead Committed in Postgres hides uncommitted writes and still allows a lost update: two transactions read stock = 1, both write stock = 0. The isolation level did not save you. A `version` column or `SELECT FOR UPDATE` does. Repeatable Read gives the transaction a snapshot, so a report does not see a row change halfway through. A concurrent update of the same row fails instead of overwriting, and your code has to retry.\n\nYou would not turn on Serializable for checkout because it sounds safer. Under contention the database aborts one of the transactions, the client retries, and the retries become the load. Name the anomaly you are stopping. If you cannot name it, you do not need the stricter level."
  },
  "optimistic-pessimistic": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Optimistic vs pessimistic locking",
    "body": "**Both stop two writers from silently clobbering one row**. They differ in when they notice.\n\nPessimistic locking takes the row lock up front: `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`. The second transaction waits or times out. You use it when collisions are likely and the critical section is short, such as decrementing the last units of stock.\n\nOptimistic locking lets both transactions read. Each write carries the version it read: `UPDATE ... SET version = version + 1 WHERE id = ? AND version = ?`. Zero rows updated means someone else won. The loser retries or tells the user. You use it when collisions are rare, such as editing a profile, because you never hold a lock across a user think-time or a remote call.\n\nThe failure of pessimistic locking is a lock held while you call a payment API. The pool fills with waiters. The failure of optimistic locking is a hot row where every writer retries. That row wants a lock, or a queue, not a version column.\n\n**Lock when collisions are the common case and the critical section is a few statements. Version the row when collisions are rare.** *Neither lock may be held across an HTTP call.*\n\nDecrementing the last unit of a flash-sale SKU is pessimistic: `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, update, commit. The second buyer waits or gets a conflict you can explain. Editing a profile is optimistic: send the version you read, update `WHERE version = ?`, and if zero rows change, show \"someone else saved.\" You never take `FOR UPDATE` and then call the payment API. That lock is held for the whole round trip, and every other buyer of that SKU queues behind it.\n\nOptimistic locking on that same hot SKU is the other failure. Every writer retries, the retries synchronize, and you have built a stampede out of a version column. That row wants a queue or a single lock, not hope."
  },
  "deadlocks": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Deadlocks",
    "body": "**A deadlock is two transactions each holding a lock the other needs**. The database detects the cycle and aborts one of them. The application must retry that one.\n\nIt happens when two requests lock the same rows in different orders. Order A then B in one request, B then A in another. The fix is a global lock order: always lock the lower id first. Keep transactions short so locks are not held across HTTP calls.\n\nYou will not design this away completely under contention. You design so the abort is rare and the retry is safe. A retry that is not idempotent will double-charge on the second attempt.\n\nHow you know: the database logs a deadlock, the request returns a serialization or deadlock error, and the retry succeeds. A rising deadlock rate means a new query is taking locks in a new order, or a transaction got slower.\n\n**Pick one lock order and keep the transaction shorter than the HTTP call it must not contain.** *The database will abort one session. Your job is to retry it once, safely.*\n\nTwo transfers, A→B and B→A, lock the account rows in opposite orders. Postgres detects the cycle, rolls one transaction back, and returns a deadlock error. The other commits. You retry the loser. If the retry inserts a second payment because you did not store an idempotency key, the deadlock fix becomes a double charge.\n\nA rising deadlock rate after a deploy usually means a new query locks a child row before the parent, or a transaction now includes a remote call. You do not \"turn deadlocks off.\" You make the order global (lower id first) and you watch the error rate. A retry storm on deadlock is the same shape as a retry storm on timeouts."
  },
  "replication-replicas": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Replication and read replicas",
    "body": "**Replication copies the write-ahead log from a primary to one or more standbys**. The primary still takes every write. Replicas absorb reads.\n\nYou add a replica when read QPS or reporting is the bottleneck and the data can be a moment stale. You do not add a replica to scale writes. All writes still land on the primary.\n\nAsync replication is the default. The primary commits, then the replica catches up. If the primary dies, the last seconds of commits can be missing. Sync replication waits for a replica and raises write latency. You pay that when losing a payment commit is unacceptable.\n\nThe bug is read-your-writes. A user saves a profile and the next request hits a replica that doesn't have it yet. Send that user's next read to the primary, or wait for the replica to pass the commit position you just wrote.\n\nFailover promotes a replica. Clients must learn the new primary. Connections to the old one fail. That window is the RTO of the database.\n\n**Replicas scale reads. They do not scale writes, and they lag.** *The user who just wrote must not be sent to a replica that has not caught up.*\n\nThe primary in one region takes every insert. A replica in the same region, and another in Europe, serve product pages and reports. Async replication means the primary commits, then the replica applies the log. A profile save followed by a read of that profile can miss. You pin that user to the primary for a second, or you pass the log position and wait until the replica has passed it. Sync replication avoids the lost tail and adds the replica's round trip to every commit. Use it for the payment ledger. Do not use it for a click counter.\n\nFailover is a promotion plus a new place for clients to connect. Until the connection string or the proxy moves, writes fail. That gap is the database RTO, and a replica that was seconds behind loses those seconds."
  },
  "partitioning-sharding": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Partitioning and sharding",
    "body": "**Both split a table so one machine does not hold all of the rows or all of the writes**. The difference is where the split lives.\n\nPartitioning is inside one database. The planner prunes partitions that cannot match the query. You use it for time-series retention: drop last month's partition instead of deleting a billion rows. Queries that include the partition key stay fast. Queries that don't, scan every partition.\n\nSharding puts partitions on different servers. You do it when one primary's disk, CPU, or write rate is actually exhausted. The application, or a proxy, routes by shard key. A query that needs every shard becomes a scatter-gather.\n\nPick the key from the access path. Orders by `customer_id` keep one customer's reads on one shard. A key that is a timestamp puts all of today's writes on one shard. That shard is the hotspot you built.\n\nCross-shard transactions are the cost. Avoid them. If two rows must commit together, they belong on the same shard, or they should not be one transaction.\n\n**Partition inside one database for retention and prune. Shard across servers only when one primary's writes are actually full.** *A query that omits the key scans every piece.*\n\nMonthly partitions on an events table let you drop June instead of deleting a billion rows. The planner skips months the `WHERE` cannot touch. A report with no date scans all of them and is slower than the unpartitioned table. Sharding starts when that primary's disk, CPU, or WAL cannot take the write rate. Route by `customer_id` so one customer's orders stay together. A shard key of `created_at` puts today's traffic on one server. You built the hotspot.\n\nCross-shard transactions are the cost that makes people regret the split. Two rows that must commit together belong on the same shard. Otherwise the business step is a saga, and you say that before you draw the second database."
  },
  "sql-vs-nosql": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "SQL vs NoSQL",
    "body": "**Choose from the access pattern and the invariant, not from fashion**.\n\nA relational database when you need transactions across rows, constraints, and ad-hoc queries. Orders, payments, and inventory fit. Postgres is the default you should be ready to defend.\n\nKey-value, such as DynamoDB or Redis, when you always get one item by a known key at high QPS and you can model the partition key so it spreads. You give up joins.\n\nA document store when the unit you read and write is one aggregate, shaped like the screen, and you rarely update a field inside millions of them independently.\n\nWide-column, such as Cassandra, when you append by a partition key, read that partition in order, and can accept tunable consistency. Time-series and activity feeds fit. Multi-row transactions do not.\n\nA graph database only when the question is the traversal itself. It is the wrong default for orders.\n\nThe interview answer is: start with Postgres until a measured bottleneck forces a second store, and then that store owns one access path, not the whole domain.\n\n**Start with a relational database until a measured access path does not fit.** *A second store owns that path. It does not become a second source of truth for the same row.*\n\nOrders, payments, and inventory need constraints and a transaction. Postgres is the answer you defend. A session or a rate-limit counter is a key lookup at a rate one primary should not serve. Redis or DynamoDB takes that path, and you accept no joins. Cassandra fits an append-only feed keyed by user, read back in order. It does not fit \"transfer money and update inventory together.\"\n\nYou would not introduce three databases in the first drawing because each one is fashionable for a piece of the noun. Every extra store is a dual-write. If you do add one, name the query it serves and the query you refuse to run on it."
  },
  "queue-vs-topic": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Queue vs topic",
    "body": "**A queue delivers each message to one consumer**. Competing workers share the work. A topic delivers each message to every subscriber. Each subscriber has its own copy.\n\nUse a queue when a job must happen once: resize this image, send this email. Use a topic, or Kafka's log with several consumer groups, when many independent systems care: billing, search, and notifications all see the same order event.\n\nKafka is a log, not a classic queue. A consumer group behaves like a queue: each partition is read by one member of the group. A second group reads the same log from its own offsets. That is how one topic feeds several pipelines without them stealing each other's messages.\n\nJMS names the same split: a queue versus a topic. The interview does not care which vendor. It cares whether a second consumer sees the message or takes it away from the first.\n\nThe mistake is a topic with a slow subscriber that blocks the publisher. In a log, a slow group does not block the others. In a broker that fans out synchronously, it does.\n\n**A queue is one worker taking the job. A topic is every interested system seeing the same fact.** *A slow subscriber must not stall the others, and it must not steal their copy.*\n\n\"Send this email\" is a queue. One consumer acks it, and the others never see it. \"An order was paid\" is a topic. Billing, search, and notifications each have a consumer group and an offset. Kafka gives you that split on one log. A second group does not take the message away from the first. JMS names the same thing: queue versus topic. The vendor is not the question.\n\nYou would not publish the order event onto a single competing-consumer queue and then expect three services to share it. One of them gets the message. The others starve. And you would not use a synchronous fan-out, where the publisher waits for every subscriber, unless you are willing to be as slow as the slowest one."
  },
  "delivery-guarantees": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Delivery guarantees",
    "body": "**At-most-once: send or process, and if you crash you may lose it**. You get this when you commit the offset before you do the work, or you publish without waiting for an ack.\n\nAt-least-once: you will not lose it, but a crash can deliver it twice. This is the Kafka default worth designing for. The consumer does the work, then commits the offset. A crash in between redelivers.\n\nExactly-once is a narrow contract. Kafka transactions can make the consume-transform-produce step happen once, inside Kafka. The moment you call a database or a payment API, you are back to at-least-once plus idempotency. Say that. Do not promise exactly-once across a third party.\n\nAcknowledgements are the lever. `acks=all` waits for replicas and loses less. `acks=0` is faster and loses data. A consumer that auto-commits on an interval will skip or repeat work depending on when it crashed.\n\nPick at-least-once and make the handler idempotent. That is the answer that survives the follow-up.\n\n**Design for at-least-once, and make the effect safe to repeat.** *Exactly-once stops at the boundary of the log. A database or a card network is outside that boundary.*\n\nCommit the offset after the database transaction, not on a timer. A crash in between redelivers. The consumer inserts the event id and applies the change in one transaction, so the second delivery is a no-op. `acks=all` with a replication factor of 3 waits for replicas and loses less. `acks=0` is how you drop events and then cannot explain the missing balance.\n\nKafka transactions can make consume-transform-produce happen once, back into Kafka. The moment you call a payment API, you are at-least-once plus an idempotency key. Say that sentence. Promising exactly-once email or exactly-once charging is the answer that falls apart on the next question."
  },
  "message-ordering": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Message ordering",
    "body": "**A queue of independent jobs does not need order**. A customer's balance events do. If \"debit\" is processed before \"account opened\", the consumer fails or corrupts state.\n\nKafka orders messages inside one partition, not across the topic. All events for one customer must use the same key so they land on the same partition, and one consumer must handle that partition. Two consumers on one partition do not exist in a single group.\n\nRetries break order if you skip ahead. If message 2 fails and you process message 3, you have reordered the partition. For a key that needs order, stop the partition and retry, or send the failure to a delay topic that still keys by customer.\n\nA broker without partitions, such as a single RabbitMQ queue with one consumer, is ordered and does not scale. The moment you add a second consumer, order across messages is gone unless you shard the queues yourself.\n\nSay the scope out loud: ordered per customer, not globally. Global order is one partition, and one partition is the throughput ceiling.\n\n**Order inside one partition, for one key, and say that scope out loud.** *Global order is one partition, and one partition is the throughput ceiling.*\n\nCustomer 42's \"opened\", \"debited\", \"credited\" share the key `customerId`. They land on one partition. One consumer in the group handles that partition, so the debit cannot overtake the open. Customer 99 is a different partition and runs in parallel. If message 2 fails and you process message 3, you have reordered the stream. Stop that partition, or retry on a delay topic that keeps the same key.\n\nA random key spreads the load and gives you no order. That is correct for image resize and wrong for a balance. Adding consumers does not order a key that was spread, and it does not speed up the one hot customer who hashes alone."
  },
  "retries-dlq": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Retries, dead letters, and poison messages",
    "body": "**A consumer fails for two different reasons, and they need different handling**.\n\nA down database is transient. Retry with backoff. The message stays, or returns to the topic, and succeeds later. A poison message is bad data: a payload that will never parse. Retrying it forever blocks the partition and the customers behind it.\n\nAfter a small number of attempts, publish the message to a dead-letter topic with the error, and commit the offset so the partition moves. Alert on the dead-letter rate. A human or a fix-up job looks at it. Do not auto-retry a poison message on the main path.\n\nThe retry must be idempotent, because some of the \"failures\" were actually successes whose ack was lost.\n\nHow you know it is working: lag is stable, and the dead-letter topic receives a trickle you can explain. A partition whose lag grows while the consumer is up, looping on one offset, is a poison message you failed to quarantine.\n\n**Retry what is down. Quarantine what will never parse.** *A poison record that stays on the partition blocks every customer behind it.*\n\nThe database is restarting. Back off and try the same record again. The payload is missing a field the code cannot invent. After a few attempts, publish it to a dead-letter topic with the error and the offset, then commit so the partition moves. Alert on the dead-letter rate. A person or a fix-up job looks at it. Do not put it back on the main topic in a loop.\n\nSome \"failures\" were successes whose ack was lost, so the retry path is the same idempotent handler. You know this is working when lag is flat and the dead letter is a trickle you can name. Lag that climbs while the consumer is up, stuck on one offset, is a poison message you failed to move."
  },
  "idempotent-consumers": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Idempotent consumers",
    "body": "**The broker will deliver a message more than once**. The consumer has to make the second delivery a no-op.\n\nThe usual table is `processed_event (event_id primary key, processed_at)`. In one transaction, insert the id and apply the business write. The second delivery hits a primary-key conflict and commits nothing new.\n\n```\nbegin\n  insert into processed_event(event_id) values (?)\n  update account set balance = balance - ? where id = ?\ncommit\n```\n\nIf the effect is naturally idempotent, you may not need the table. Setting status to SHIPPED twice is fine. Adding 1 to a counter twice is not.\n\nDeduplication has a window. If you keep ids for a day and the broker redelivers after a week, you will apply it again. Keep ids at least as long as you can redeliver, which means at least your retention.\n\nDo the check in the same transaction as the write. A check, then a commit, then a write, races with the duplicate.\n\n**The event id and the business write commit together, or the duplicate still wins.** *Dedup keys have to live at least as long as the broker can redeliver.*\n\n`processed_event(event_id)` is a primary key. The consumer inserts that id and updates the balance in one transaction. The second delivery hits the key and rolls back the extra update. Setting a shipment to SHIPPED twice needs no table. Adding one to a counter twice does. A check in one transaction and the write in another races: both deliveries pass the check, both write.\n\nKeep the ids for the retention of the topic, not for an hour, if the topic will still redeliver next week. A consumer that \"remembers\" ids in memory forgets them on restart, which is exactly when the redelivery arrives."
  },
  "kafka-architecture": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Kafka architecture",
    "body": "**A topic is a named log, split into partitions**. A partition is an ordered, append-only file on a broker. The partition has a leader and followers. Producers append. Consumers read at their own pace.\n\nA consumer group is a set of processes sharing the topic. Kafka assigns each partition to one member. If you have 12 partitions and 4 consumers, each consumer gets about 3. If you have 4 partitions and 12 consumers, 8 sit idle. Adding consumers does not help past the partition count.\n\nThe offset is the consumer's position, stored for the group. Committing offset 40 means \"I have handled everything before 40.\" Lag is the distance between the latest offset and the committed one.\n\nReplication is copies of the partition on other brokers. If the leader dies, a follower takes over. `acks=all` plus a replication factor of 3 and `min.insync.replicas=2` is the usual durability story.\n\nRebalancing happens when a member joins or leaves. Partitions move. A stop-the-world rebalance pauses that group. A long processing time and a short session timeout causes needless rebalances.\n\nRetention deletes old segments by time or size. It is what lets consumers be down for 30 minutes and still catch up. If retention is shorter than the outage, the backlog is gone.\n\nOrdering, lag, and retention are one story: key into a partition, watch the lag, and keep the log longer than the longest outage you intend to survive.\n\n**Partitions set the parallelism, the key sets the order, and retention sets how long a consumer may be gone.** *Adding consumers past the partition count does nothing. Eight of twelve will sit idle.*\n\nTwelve partitions and four consumers means about three partitions each. The offset is that group's position. Lag is the distance from the tip, per partition, not as an average that hides one hot customer. Replication factor 3, `acks=all`, and `min.insync.replicas=2` is the durability you can say without hand-waving. A rebalance runs when a member dies or joins. A processing loop longer than the poll interval looks like a dead member and rebalances the group that was only slow.\n\nRetention is the 30-minute outage. If the log expires in ten minutes, the backlog is gone and no consumer count brings it back. Watch lag per partition and the age of the oldest uncommitted offset."
  },
  "partitioning-strategy": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Partitioning strategy",
    "body": "**The partition key is the most important Kafka decision, because it fixes both order and the ceiling of one key**.\n\n`customerId` as the key puts every event for that customer in one partition. One consumer sees them in order. A different customer's events are elsewhere and are not ordered relative to the first. That is what you want for \"ordering matters per customer.\"\n\nA random key or a round-robin spread maximizes throughput and gives you no order. Use it for independent jobs: image resize, email send.\n\nA bad key is a constant, or a country when one country is 90% of traffic. That partition becomes the hotspot. The rest of the cluster is idle. You cannot fix it by adding consumers. You change the key, and old messages stay on the old partition.\n\nIf one customer really is huge, give that customer a dedicated topic or add a second-level shard inside the key, and accept that those sub-streams are only ordered within themselves.\n\nCount partitions for peak throughput per partition, not for today's consumer count. Raising the count later does not re-partition old keys.\n\n**The key is the product decision, not a throughput knob you twist later.** *One customer who is half the traffic is half of one partition, and more partitions will not move them.*\n\n`customerId` keeps per-customer order and accepts that customer's rate as a ceiling. A round-robin key is for work that is independent. A constant key, or a country code when one country dominates, is a hotspot you can see as one partition's lag while the cluster looks idle. Changing the key later does not move old records. New messages hash differently. The two schemes do not meet.\n\nIf one tenant is genuinely huge, give them a dedicated topic or a second shard inside the key, and tell the interviewer those sub-streams are only ordered within themselves. Size the partition count for peak bytes per partition, not for today's consumer process count."
  },
  "broker-choice": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Broker choice",
    "body": "**Pick the broker from the retention and the number of readers, not from the logo**.\n\nKafka, or a managed equivalent such as MSK, when several teams consume the same event, you need to replay, and order per key matters. The log is the product. Operational cost is partitions, lag, and disk.\n\nRabbitMQ, and the JMS queue model it matches, when you have a task queue: one message, one worker, and you want routing keys and per-message ack. It is a poor replay log. ActiveMQ sits in the same family, often where a company already speaks JMS.\n\nSQS when you want a managed queue, at-least-once, and you do not need order or replay. FIFO queues add order and a throughput cap. SNS in front of SQS is the fan-out: one event, many queues, each with its own consumer.\n\nJMS is the API, not the bus. A `Queue` is competing consumers. A `Topic` is fan-out. If the interviewer says JMS, answer with that split, then say which modern broker you would run under it.\n\nThe wrong choice is Kafka for a single email worker, or SQS when three services must replay the last day of orders.\n\n**Pick from how many systems must read the event, and whether you must replay it.** *A task that one worker must do once is a queue. A fact that three teams must each handle is a log.*\n\nKafka or a managed equivalent when billing, search, and notifications all consume the order, order per customer matters, and you may replay the day. The cost is disk, partitions, and lag. RabbitMQ, or a JMS queue, when you want competing workers, routing keys, and per-message ack, and you do not need to rewind. SQS when you want that queue managed and you can live without replay. SNS in front of SQS is the fan-out if you do not want to run a log. FIFO SQS adds order and a cap.\n\nJMS is the API. A `Queue` is competing consumers. A `Topic` is fan-out. You would not stand up Kafka so one email worker has a place to poll."
  },
  "cache-aside": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Cache-aside",
    "body": "**The application talks to the cache and the database**. The cache does not.\n\nOn a read, look in Redis. On a miss, read the database and set the key with a TTL. On a write, update the database, then delete the key. The next read fills it. You delete rather than update so two writers don't race to set different values.\n\n```\nvalue = cache.get(key)\nif value is missing:\n    value = db.load(key)\n    cache.set(key, value, ttl)\n```\n\nYou choose this when the cache is an optimization and the database is the source of truth. You reject write-through when you don't want every write to pay cache latency, and you reject read-through when you don't want a cache proxy in the middle.\n\nThe failure is a stale fill: a reader loaded the old row, a writer committed and deleted the key, then the reader sets the old value. A short TTL bounds it. Deleting after the commit, not before, narrows the window. If Redis is down, read the database and do not fail the request.\n\n**The database is the source of truth. The cache is a delete-on-write optimization.** *If Redis is down, read the database. Do not fail the request, and do not retry the cache in a loop.*\n\nThe race worth naming: a reader loads the old row, a writer commits and deletes the key, then the reader sets the old value back. A short TTL bounds how long that lie lives. Deleting after the commit, not before, narrows the window. You delete rather than update so two writers cannot each set a different \"new\" value.\n\nYou would not use cache-aside for a read-after-write the same user must see instantly unless you also send that user's next read to the database. And you would not fill the cache from a replica that has not caught up. That plants the stale row on purpose."
  },
  "read-through": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Read-through",
    "body": "**The application asks only the cache**. On a miss, the cache itself loads from the database and stores the value. The caller cannot tell a hit from a filled miss.\n\nYou choose it when many clients must share one loading policy and you don't want each service to reimplement cache-aside. A caching proxy or a library with a loader function does this.\n\n*You reject it when the load is not a simple key lookup: a query with several filters, or a load that must see the caller's transaction*. The cache cannot run that safely.\n\nWrites are not part of this pattern. Pair it with write-through or with an explicit invalidation. If you only read-through and never invalidate, the value lives until TTL, and the interviewer will ask how stale that is.\n\nIf the loader's database call fails, the cache must not store the error as a value for the full TTL. A short negative cache is a separate, deliberate choice.\n\n**One loader, shared by every client, and only for a lookup the cache can do alone.** *A miss that throws must not be stored as the value.*\n\nYou want this when several services would otherwise copy the same get-or-load code and drift. A library calls your loader on a miss and stores the result. The caller cannot tell a hit from a fill. You do not want it when the load depends on the caller's transaction or on a filter the cache cannot see. The cache would return a row the transaction should not have seen.\n\nWrites are a different pattern. Read-through without an invalidation or a TTL is a value that never changes. If the loader's database call fails, store nothing, or store a very short negative entry on purpose. Caching the exception for the full TTL turns a blip into a long outage for that key."
  },
  "write-through": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Write-through",
    "body": "**The application writes to the cache, and the cache writes to the database before acknowledging**. A later read hits the value just written. The database and the cache move together.\n\nYou choose it when a read-after-write must be fresh and you can pay the database latency on the write path. Session state that is read on the next request is a fair example.\n\n*You reject cache-aside here because cache-aside deletes and refetches, and a second request can still miss*. You reject write-behind because a crash of the cache loses writes that were acknowledged.\n\nThe cost is write latency: the user waits for both stores. The cache is not a buffer. If the database write fails, the cache write must fail too, or you have a value that will never be reloaded correctly.\n\nIt does not protect you from a second cache cluster in another region. That other region is still stale until replication or TTL.\n\n**Acknowledge the write only after the database has it, and update the cache in that same success.** *A cache ack without a database commit is a value you cannot reload.*\n\nSession data that the next request must see is a fair use. The user waits for both stores. That latency is the cost of not serving a stale session. If the database write fails, the cache write fails too. Otherwise the next read hits a value the database will never confirm, and a later fill overwrites it with the old row.\n\nYou would not pick write-through to \"make the cache faster.\" It makes the write slower than cache-aside. And it does nothing for a second region. That region's cache is still on its own TTL until an invalidation crosses the network."
  },
  "write-behind": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Write-behind",
    "body": "**The application writes to the cache and gets an ack**. The cache flushes to the database later, in batches. Write latency looks like cache latency. The database sees a smoother, smaller write rate.\n\nYou choose it for high write rates where losing a few seconds is acceptable: view counts, presence, a draft that is also saved elsewhere. You reject it for payments and orders. An acknowledged write that is only in memory is not durable.\n\nThe failure is a cache crash before the flush. Those writes are gone. Replication of the cache reduces the window and does not close it unless the flush has happened.\n\nReaders of the cache see fresh data. Readers of the database, including a report and a replica, see the lag. Say that split out loud. If a process bypasses the cache and reads the database, it will make a decision on stale state.\n\nBackpressure matters. If the flush falls behind, the cache is holding a growing dirty set. At some point you stop accepting writes.\n\n**Ack from the cache only when losing the dirty set is acceptable.** *Payments and orders are not that case. A crash before the flush drops acknowledged writes.*\n\nView counts and presence fit. The database sees a batch later, so the write rate looks smooth. Readers of Redis see the new count. A report that reads the database does not, until the flush. Say that split. A process that bypasses the cache and decides from the database is deciding on the lag.\n\nReplication of Redis shrinks the window and does not close it. If the flush falls behind, the dirty set grows and you stop accepting writes. That backpressure is the design. An unbounded dirty set is how the cache becomes the only copy of an hour of counts and then restarts empty."
  },
  "cache-invalidation": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Cache invalidation",
    "body": "**Invalidation is how a write tells the cache that a key is wrong**. The reliable version is delete-on-write. The next read reloads from the database.\n\nUpdating the key in place is faster and easier to get wrong. Two writers read the old value, both compute a new one, and the slower write puts the stale value back. A delete loses that race less often, at the cost of a miss.\n\nYou cannot invalidate a key you cannot name. A page cached under many query strings, or a CDN URL with no link back to the row, will stay stale until TTL. Design the key so a write knows it: `product:42`, not a hash of the whole HTML.\n\nCross-service invalidation is the hard form. The service that writes the row must publish \"product 42 changed\" or call the cache. If it forgets, every TTL in the system is your real consistency bound. Say the number.\n\n**Delete the key you can name, in the same flow as the commit.** *A page you cannot address from the write stays stale until TTL, so the TTL is the consistency you actually have.*\n\n`product:42` is a key the order service can delete. A CDN URL that hashes the whole HTML is not. Two writers that read-modify-write the cached JSON will put the slower one's value back. A delete loses that race less often, at the cost of one miss. Retry the delete if the cache call fails after the commit. If you cannot, you are serving the old price until the key expires. Pick that number on purpose and say it.\n\nCross-service, the writer publishes \"product 42 changed\" or the cache is wrong. Hoping every service remembers is not a strategy."
  },
  "ttl": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "TTL",
    "body": "**A TTL is the maximum time you are willing to serve a wrong value, and the maximum time a key occupies memory after it stops being read**.\n\nSet it from the business, not from a default. A product price might tolerate 30 seconds. A user's session might live 30 minutes of idle time. A feature flag might be 10 seconds because a stuck flag is an incident.\n\nTTL is not a substitute for invalidation when the write must be visible now. It is the safety net when invalidation is missed, and the only mechanism when you cannot name the key.\n\nA single TTL for every key means they expire together if they were written together. That is a stampede. Jitter the TTL by a random fraction so a deploy that fills the cache does not empty it in the same second.\n\nToo long, and you debug ghosts. Too short, and the database sees the full read rate. The hit ratio tells you which one you have.\n\n**The TTL is the longest you will serve a value you know might be wrong.** *It is not a substitute for invalidation when the next screen must show the write.*\n\nA price might be 30 seconds. A feature flag might be 10, because a stuck flag is an incident. A session idle time might be 30 minutes. One TTL for every key filled in the same deploy means they expire together, and the database sees a stampede. Jitter by a random fraction. The hit ratio tells you which mistake you made: too long and you debug ghosts, too short and the database sees the full read rate.\n\nYou would not set a day-long TTL on a permission. Revocation would be a day late, and the TTL would be the authorization bug."
  },
  "eviction": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Eviction",
    "body": "**When the cache is full, something has to go**. The policy is which something.\n\nLRU evicts the key that has not been read for the longest time. It fits a workload where recent items are likely to be read again: session data, the product page that is trending. It fails on a scan that touches every key once and flushes the real working set.\n\nLFU evicts the least often used. It protects a small set of very hot keys from a flood of one-time keys. It can get stuck on a key that was hot yesterday and is cold today, unless the frequency decays.\n\nRedis defaults to a configurable policy. `allkeys-lru` is the usual choice when the cache is allowed to drop any key. A cache of durable data should not use a no-eviction policy. Writes will fail when memory is full, and that failure becomes user-facing.\n\nEviction is not invalidation. An evicted key is reloaded on the next miss. A wrong value that is still hot stays until TTL or an explicit delete.\n\n**Eviction drops a key you can reload. It does not correct a key that is wrong.** *A no-eviction cache of disposable data fails writes when memory is full, and that failure becomes the user's error.*\n\nLRU fits sessions and a trending product, and it fails when a scan touches every key once and flushes the working set. LFU protects a small hot set from a flood of one-time keys, and it can cling to yesterday's celebrity unless the count decays. `allkeys-lru` is the usual Redis choice when any key may go. The next miss reloads it. A hot wrong value stays until TTL or an explicit delete, because eviction prefers cold keys.\n\nSize the memory for the working set you measured, not for the whole catalog. The tail should miss."
  },
  "cache-stampede": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Cache stampede",
    "body": "**A hot key expires**. A thousand requests miss at once. All of them hit the database with the same expensive query. The database falls over, the cache never refills, and the miss storm continues.\n\nYou prevent it by making sure one request rebuilds the key. The others wait or serve the previous value. A lock per key, with a short timeout, is the straightforward version. A stale-while-revalidate approach serves the expired value and refreshes in the background, so users never wait on the rebuild.\n\nJittered TTLs stop many keys from expiring in the same second. They do not save you from one key that everyone wants.\n\nIf the rebuild itself is slow, the waiters need a timeout that fails soft: return a slightly stale value or a degraded page, not a pile of threads blocked on the lock. Those threads are how the stampede spreads into the application.\n\n**One request rebuilds a hot key. The others wait or serve the previous value.** *A thousand misses on one expiry will take the database down, and the cache will never refill.*\n\nA per-key lock with a short timeout is the simple version. Stale-while-revalidate serves the expired value and refreshes in the background, so the user never waits on the rebuild. Jittered TTLs stop a whole namespace expiring in the same second. They do not save the one key everyone wants, the front-page product.\n\nIf the rebuild is slow, waiters need a timeout that fails soft: a slightly stale page or a smaller response, not a pile of threads blocked on the lock. Those threads are how the stampede leaves Redis and fills the API. You would not \"fix\" it by disabling the TTL. Then the key never refreshes, and a bad value lives until a deploy."
  },
  "cache-penetration": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Cache penetration",
    "body": "**Penetration is a miss on a key that does not exist in the database either**. An attacker, or a buggy client, requests random ids. Every request misses the cache and hits the database. The cache never helps, because you don't store the miss.\n\nCache the absence. Store a short-lived \"not found\" for that key, long enough to absorb the burst and short enough that a newly created row appears quickly.\n\nA bloom filter in front of the cache answers \"definitely not present\" without a database round trip. It is worth it when the id space is huge and the valid set is known. It is extra machinery when a negative cache entry would do.\n\nValidate the shape of the id before either store. A key that cannot exist is not a cache problem. It is a request you reject at the edge.\n\nPenetration is not a stampede. A stampede is many clients rebuilding one real key. Penetration is many keys that were never real.\n\n**Cache the absence, briefly, and reject ids that cannot exist before either store.** *A miss on a key the database does not have is a query the cache will never absorb unless you store the miss.*\n\nRandom ids from a buggy client or an attacker miss Redis and hit Postgres every time. Store a short \"not found\" so the burst dies and a row created a minute later can still appear. A bloom filter answers \"definitely absent\" without a round trip when the valid set is huge and known. It is extra machinery when a negative entry would do.\n\nThis is not a stampede. A stampede is many clients rebuilding one real key. Penetration is many keys that were never real. Validate the shape at the edge so a garbage id never becomes a cache key."
  },
  "hot-keys": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Hot keys",
    "body": "**A hot key is one slot that takes a disproportionate share of traffic: a celebrity profile, a flash-sale product, a config key every request reads**.\n\nSharding Redis does not spread one key. The key hashes to one slot, and that slot's CPU saturates while the cluster looks idle. The fix is to split the key or to stop hitting it.\n\nFor a read-mostly key, keep a copy in the process for a second. A million requests a second become one Redis read per instance per second. The value can be a second stale. Say so.\n\nFor a write-heavy counter, split it into `views:42:0` through `views:42:N` and sum them when you read. Writes spread. The read is more expensive and rarer.\n\nThe failure mode of a local copy is a stampeded reload when the local TTL expires on every instance at once. Jitter it. The failure mode of splitting writes is a read that forgets a shard and undercounts.\n\n**One key hashes to one slot. More Redis nodes do not spread it.** *A local copy is allowed to be a second stale. Say the second.*\n\nA celebrity profile or a config key read on every request saturates one shard while the cluster looks idle. Keep a process-local copy for a second so a million QPS becomes one Redis read per instance per second. Jitter that local TTL or every instance refreshes together and you have rebuilt the stampede. A write-heavy counter splits into `views:42:0` through `views:42:N`. Writes spread. The read sums them and is rarer. Forgetting a shard undercounts.\n\nYou would not shard the key by a random suffix if the caller needs a consistent read of one value. They would get a random replica of the truth."
  },
  "distributed-cache": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Distributed cache",
    "body": "**A distributed cache is a cluster of nodes that looks like one map**. The client hashes the key to a node. Redis Cluster and similar systems add replicas of each slot.\n\nYou use it when the working set or the QPS does not fit one cache process. You still have one network hop, so it is not faster than a well-sized local cache. It is shared across app instances, which a local cache is not.\n\nReplication inside the cache is for surviving a node loss, not for durability of business data. If the cache is empty after a failover, the database must be able to take the miss storm, or you warm the cache first.\n\nClients need the topology. A stale map sends keys to the wrong node and you get misses or errors. Use a client that follows redirects.\n\n*Do not put the only copy of a write in the cache unless you have accepted write-behind and its data loss*. The source of truth stays in the database.\n\n**A cluster shares the working set across app instances. It is not a durable database and it is not faster than a hot local copy.** *After a failover the cache can be empty. The database has to survive that miss storm, or you warm first.*\n\nThe client hashes the key to a slot. Replicas of the slot are there so a node loss does not erase the key immediately. They are not a commit log. A stale topology map sends keys to the wrong node. Use a client that follows redirects. One network hop remains, so a value read on every request still wants a local cache in front.\n\n*Do not put the only copy of a write in the cluster unless you have accepted write-behind and the loss that comes with it*. The source of truth stays in the database, and the cache is allowed to be wrong for the TTL you named."
  },
  "cache-consistency": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Cache consistency",
    "body": "**The cache and the database will disagree**. The design question is for how long, and who can see it.\n\nAfter a write, the same user should usually see the new value. Delete the key in the same flow as the commit, and read that user's follow-up from the database if you have to. Other users can see the old value until the next fill. That is the normal cache-aside bargain.\n\nA write that succeeds in the database and fails to delete the key leaves a stale entry until TTL. Retry the delete. If you cannot, the TTL is your consistency bound. Pick it on purpose.\n\nTwo regions make this worse. Each region has a cache. Invalidation has to cross the network. If it doesn't, region B serves the old price until TTL. For a price, that may be unacceptable, and the answer is not to cache it at the edge.\n\n*Don't talk about the cache as strongly consistent*. Talk about the window.\n\n**Name the window. Do not call the cache strongly consistent.** *The same user usually must see their write. Everyone else can wait until the next fill.*\n\nDelete the key in the same flow as the commit. If that user's next read cannot tolerate a miss that refills from a lagging replica, send it to the primary. A write that commits and then fails to delete the key is stale until TTL. Retry the delete. The TTL is the bound you are left with. Pick it.\n\nTwo regions mean two caches. Invalidation has to cross the link. If it does not, the other region serves the old price until expiry. For a price that must match, do not cache it at the edge. You would not add a synchronous cross-region cache invalidation on the checkout path and then be surprised that every write waits on the far region."
  },
  "redis-structures": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Redis data structures",
    "body": "**Redis is not only a string map**. The structure is the feature.\n\nStrings hold a cached JSON blob or a counter (`INCR`). Hashes hold the fields of one object so you can change one field without rewriting the value. Lists and streams hold a queue or a log. Sets hold unique members, useful for \"which of these ids are in the cart.\" Sorted sets hold a score, which is a leaderboard or a rate-limit window of timestamps.\n\nYou pick a structure because the operation you need is atomic on the server. Incrementing a counter in a string you read-modify-write from the client races. `INCR` does not. Checking a sliding window by sending the whole list to the app races. A Lua script or a sorted-set prune does not.\n\nThe cost of the clever structure is memory and a hot key. A sorted set of every event for a celebrity is one key on one shard. A cache of HTML under a string key has the same problem if everyone wants that one page.\n\nExpiry is per key, not per field in a hash, unless you encode time into the structure yourself.\n\n**Pick the structure whose operation is atomic on the server.** *A read-modify-write of a string from the client races. `INCR` and a Lua script do not.*\n\nA cached JSON blob is a string. The fields of one object that you change independently are a hash, with the caveat that expiry is per key, not per field. A sliding-window rate limit is a sorted set of timestamps you prune in the same script that counts them. Sending the whole list to the app to prune it races with the next request.\n\nThe clever structure is still one key. A sorted set of every event for a celebrity is a hot slot, same as a hot string. Memory is the other bill: hashes of large objects and sets that never expire grow until eviction or an outage. Put a TTL on the key, or trim the structure, on purpose."
  },
  "cache-failure-walkthrough": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Client, API, cache, database",
    "body": "**Draw the path, then fail each box**.\n\n```\nClient --> API --> Cache --> Database\n```\n\nThe API is down. The load balancer stops sending traffic to that instance. Other instances serve. If all of them are down, the client retries and then sees an error. The cache and the database are idle and fine.\n\nThe cache is down. The API catches the timeout and reads the database. Latency goes up. If the database cannot absorb the full read rate, you shed load: serve a degraded response, or rate-limit, rather than taking the database down too. A cache failure must not be a retry loop.\n\nThe database is down. Cache hits still succeed. Misses fail. Writes fail. Say that clearly: a cache does not keep a write path alive. If you need reads during a database failover, you are serving stale hits on purpose, and new keys return an error.\n\nThe client retries. Every retry that misses multiplies database load. Timeouts on the API must be shorter than the client's retry budget, and the write must be idempotent or the retry creates a second order.\n\nWhat you watch: cache hit ratio, database QPS, and API latency together. Hit ratio falling and database QPS rising is the cache already failing, before the cache process looks down.\n\n**Fail one box at a time, and say what still works.** *A cache miss storm is not a reason to retry. A cache does not keep writes alive when the database is down.*\n\nThe API instance dies. The balancer stops using it. Other instances serve. The cache and the database are fine. The cache dies. The API reads the database and latency rises. If the database cannot take the full read rate, you shed load or serve a degraded page. You do not retry Redis until the threads are gone. The database dies. Hits still return. Misses and writes fail. New keys error. Serving stale hits during failover is a choice you state, not an accident.\n\nThe client will retry. Every retry that misses multiplies database load, and a write without an idempotency key becomes a second order. Watch hit ratio, database QPS, and API latency together. Hit ratio down and database QPS up means the cache is already failing before the process looks down."
  },
  "vertical-horizontal": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Vertical vs horizontal scaling",
    "body": "**Vertical scaling is a bigger machine**. You do it when one process is the limit and you can take a restart: more CPU for a database primary, more memory for a cache. It is simple and it ends. There is a largest box, and a single box is still one failure domain.\n\nHorizontal scaling is more copies. It works when the work is stateless or can be partitioned. API servers behind a load balancer are the usual case. You add a copy without making each copy bigger.\n\nThe app tier scales horizontally only if a request can land on any instance. Session data in memory, local disk uploads, and in-process caches break that. Move that state to Redis, a database, or object storage first.\n\nThe database does not scale the same way. More app servers increase the write load on one primary. Horizontal app scaling without a plan for the data tier just moves the bottleneck and makes it louder.\n\n**Scale the app out only after a request can land on any instance. Scale the database up, or split it, on purpose.** *More app servers make a single primary louder. They do not give it more write capacity.*\n\nVertical is a bigger box: more CPU for a primary, more memory for Redis. It is simple, it needs a restart, and it ends at the largest machine, which is still one failure domain. Horizontal is more API processes behind a balancer. That works when session state, uploads, and in-process caches have been moved to Redis, a database, or object storage. A session in memory pins the user and breaks the copy you just added.\n\nYou would not autoscaling the stateless tier while the primary is at 90% CPU and call it a scaling plan. The next step is a replica for reads, a queue for writes, or a shard. Name which."
  },
  "autoscaling": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Autoscaling",
    "body": "**Autoscaling adds or removes instances from a signal: CPU, request count, queue depth, or consumer lag**. The signal has to match the bottleneck. CPU-based scaling does nothing for an app stuck on a database pool. Lag-based scaling is what you want for consumers.\n\nScale-out is slower than the spike if the new instance needs minutes to boot and warm caches. Set a minimum size that survives the first minutes of peak, and scale on a leading signal (requests, lag) rather than CPU after the process is already late.\n\nScale-in can drop an instance that is mid-request. Connection draining has to finish first. A consumer that is removed must finish or give back its partitions.\n\nAutoscaling does not fix a hot key or a single-threaded primary. You will buy more app servers that all wait on the same row. Look at the dependency before you raise the max instance count.\n\n**Scale on the signal that matches the bottleneck, and keep a minimum that survives the first minutes.** *CPU does nothing for an app stuck waiting on a database pool. Lag is the signal for consumers.*\n\nA new instance that needs two minutes to boot and warm is late for a spike. Set the floor high enough for the start of peak, and scale on requests or queue depth rather than on CPU after you are already late. Scale-in has to drain. Killing an instance mid-request, or a consumer mid-partition, is a 502 or a redelivery.\n\nAutoscaling will not split a hot key or a single-threaded primary. You will buy more processes that all wait on the same row. Look at the dependency before you raise the maximum. A maximum that is high enough to overwhelm the database is not safety. It is how the app tier takes the database with it."
  },
  "queue-load-leveling": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Queue-based load leveling",
    "body": "**A spike that the database cannot absorb goes into a queue**. Producers append. Consumers drain at a rate the database can sustain. The user gets a 202, not a finished result.\n\nYou use it for work that can finish later: image processing, email, report generation, a checkout that reserves stock and confirms payment asynchronously. You do not use it for a request that must return the answer in the same HTTP call, unless you are willing to poll or push the result.\n\nThe queue is the buffer, so its depth is the backlog. If consumers are slower than producers forever, the queue grows until disk or retention gives up. That is not leveling. That is an outage with a delay. Cap the queue or shed at the producer when depth crosses a line.\n\nA retried enqueue must not create two jobs. The message id is the idempotency key on the consumer.\n\n**Put the spike in a queue and drain at the rate the database can take. The HTTP call returns 202, not the finished work.** *A queue that grows forever is an outage with a delay, not leveling.*\n\nImage work, email, and a report fit. A screen that must show the answer in the same call does not, unless the client polls or you push. Depth is the backlog. If consumers are slower than producers for the rest of the day, cap the queue or shed at the producer when depth crosses a line. Disk filling up is the failure mode you are trying to avoid.\n\nA retried enqueue uses the same message id the consumer already treats as an idempotency key. Otherwise the spike's retries become two jobs per request, and the database sees twice the load you thought you had leveled."
  },
  "cdn": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "CDN",
    "body": "**A CDN caches bytes close to the user**. It fits images, video, CSS, and public API responses that are the same for everyone. It does not fit a response that depends on the caller unless you vary the cache key on the right header and accept a lower hit ratio.\n\nThe origin still has to survive a miss storm. A new deploy that changes every URL, or a TTL that expires together, sends all users to the origin at once. Version the asset URL so deploys don't invalidate the world, and jitter TTLs on HTML if you cache it.\n\nInvalidation is slow and eventually consistent. For a price or a permission, do not cache at the edge. For a product image, invalidate by URL or wait for TTL.\n\nYou know it is working from the hit ratio and origin request rate. A hit ratio collapse with origin latency climbing is the CDN no longer protecting you.\n\n**Cache bytes that are the same for everyone, close to them. Do not cache a price or a permission at the edge.** *Invalidation is slow. A TTL you can live with is the real bound.*\n\nImages, video, and CSS belong on the CDN. A response that depends on the caller needs the right header in the cache key, and the hit ratio drops. The origin still has to survive a miss storm: a deploy that changes every URL, or a TTL that expires together. Version the asset URL so a deploy does not invalidate the world.\n\nYou know it is working from hit ratio and origin request rate. A collapse in the hit ratio with origin latency climbing means the CDN is no longer protecting you. You would not put the checkout API behind a long-lived edge cache to save the database. You would cache the public catalog and let checkout hit the origin."
  },
  "hotspots": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Hotspots",
    "body": "**A hotspot is a shard, partition, or row that takes far more traffic than its neighbors**. The cluster graphs look healthy. One key is on fire.\n\nCauses: a partition key that is a timestamp, a celebrity id, a flash-sale SKU, a Kafka key that is constant. Adding machines does not move that key.\n\nFixes depend on the kind of heat. A read-hot key can be cached, including a copy inside the process. A write-hot counter can be split into several keys and summed later. A hot database row can be queued so writers serialize without a thousand lock waits. A hot Kafka partition needs a different key, and you accept a smaller ordering scope.\n\nDetect it from per-key or per-partition metrics, not from the average. Average CPU of 20% and one partition at 100% is a hotspot.\n\n**A hotspot is one key, row, or partition. The average will look fine.** *Adding machines does not move a key that hashes to one place.*\n\nA timestamp shard key, a celebrity id, a flash-sale SKU, a constant Kafka key. Read-hot data can be cached, including a copy in the process. A write-hot counter can be split and summed later. A hot row can be queued so writers serialize without a thousand lock waits. A hot Kafka partition needs a different key, and you accept a smaller ordering scope.\n\nDetect it from per-key or per-partition metrics. Average CPU of 20% and one partition at 100% is the picture. You would not rebalance the whole cluster and expect that one SKU to spread. It will not."
  },
  "l4-vs-l7": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "L4 vs L7 load balancing",
    "body": "**L4 balances TCP connections**. It does not read HTTP. It is cheap and it cannot route by URL or header. An AWS NLB is this class. You use it for raw throughput, TLS passthrough, or protocols that are not HTTP.\n\nL7 balances HTTP requests. It can route `/api` to one service and `/static` to another, terminate TLS, and retry an idempotent GET. An AWS ALB, Nginx, HAProxy, Envoy, and a Kubernetes Ingress or Gateway are this class. The cost is that it parses the request and can become its own bottleneck.\n\nEnvoy is the L7 proxy you meet in a service mesh. Nginx and HAProxy are the ones you put at the edge. You do not need their config syntax in the interview. You need to say which decisions require seeing the HTTP request.\n\nL4 cannot do path-based routing or header-based deploys. If the design needs a canary by header, it is L7.\n\n**L4 moves connections. L7 reads the request and can choose a service from it.** *Path-based routing, header canaries, and retrying a GET require L7. Raw throughput and TLS passthrough fit L4.*\n\nAn NLB does not look at HTTP. It is cheap and it cannot send `/api` one way and `/static` another. An ALB, Nginx, HAProxy, or Envoy can. Envoy is the L7 proxy you meet in a mesh. The cost is that parsing the request makes the proxy a bottleneck of its own. You do not need their config syntax. You need to say which decision requires seeing the HTTP.\n\nYou would not terminate TLS and route by header on an L4 balancer and then wonder why the canary never happens. If the design needs a header, it is L7, and the health check is still what removes a bad target."
  },
  "lb-algorithms": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Balancing algorithms",
    "body": "**Round-robin sends each new request to the next healthy instance**. It is fair when every request costs about the same and every instance is equal.\n\nLeast connections sends to the instance with the fewest in-flight requests. It is better when request time varies, because round-robin will keep handing work to an instance stuck on a slow call.\n\nWeighted routing sends more traffic to bigger instances or to a canary that should only see 5%. The weight is a policy, not a measure of health.\n\nNone of these look inside a request. A shard-aware route is not least-connections. If user A must hit a specific node, that is consistent hashing or a gateway rule, and you have given up free rebalancing.\n\nPick least connections for a typical API, weighted for a canary, and say the health check is what removes a node from the set the algorithm sees.\n\n**Least connections for a typical API, weighted for a canary, and a health check underneath both.** *None of these look inside the request. A user who must hit one node is a different mechanism.*\n\nRound-robin is fair when every request costs the same. It keeps handing work to an instance stuck on a slow call. Least connections avoids that. A weight of 5% is a policy for a canary, not a measure of health. Consistent hashing or a gateway rule is how user A stays on a node that holds their state, and you have given up free rebalancing.\n\nYou would not invent a custom algorithm for \"fairness\" before the health check is right. A dead instance that stays in the set makes every algorithm look random."
  },
  "consistent-hashing": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Consistent hashing",
    "body": "**Consistent hashing maps a key to a node on a ring, so the same key keeps landing on the same node**. When you add or remove a node, only the keys on the neighboring arc move. A naive `hash % N` remaps almost every key when N changes, which is a cache flush or a data move.\n\nYou use it for a cache cluster, a sharding proxy, or sticky routing where the node holds state for a key. Virtual nodes on the ring keep one physical machine from owning a huge arc.\n\nIt does not solve a hot key. A celebrity still hashes to one node. Replication of that one key, or splitting it, is a different fix.\n\nWhen a node dies, its keys move to a neighbor and that neighbor has a cold cache or must rebuild the shard. The ring limits how much moves. It does not make the move free.\n\n**The same key stays on the same node, and a membership change moves only the neighboring keys.** *It does not spread a hot key. A celebrity still hashes to one place.*\n\n`hash % N` remaps almost everything when you add a node, which is a cache flush or a data move. A ring moves one arc. Virtual nodes stop one machine owning a huge arc. You use it for a cache cluster or a shard map. When a node dies, its keys land on a neighbor that has a cold cache or must rebuild. The ring limits how much moves. The move is not free.\n\nYou would not use consistent hashing as a session stickiness substitute and then forget replication. The node that owns the key is still one failure, and the next node does not have the memory you left behind."
  },
  "health-checks-failover": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Health checks and failover",
    "body": "**A load balancer only sends traffic to instances that pass a check**. A shallow check opens a TCP port. A deep check hits `/health` and maybe confirms the process can reach its database. Deep checks are more honest and more dangerous: if the database blips, every instance fails the check and the balancer has nowhere to send traffic.\n\nSeparate liveness from readiness. Liveness answers \"should you restart me.\" Readiness answers \"should you send users.\" A process draining or still warming is alive and not ready.\n\nFailover is taking the bad copy out and using another. For app servers that is automatic. For a database primary it is a promotion, and clients must discover the new endpoint. The time to detect, promote, and reconnect is the downtime users feel.\n\nA check that always returns 200 while the app is wedged will keep sending traffic. The check has to fail when you would want the instance removed.\n\n**Liveness restarts you. Readiness removes you from the balancer. They are not the same check.** *A deep check that includes the database will mark every instance bad when the database blips, and the balancer will have nowhere to send traffic.*\n\nA TCP check is shallow and will keep sending users to a process that is wedged but listening. `/health` should fail when you would want the instance removed, and it should not fail just because a dependency you can degrade is slow. App failover is taking the bad copy out. Database failover is a promotion, and clients must learn the new endpoint. Detect, promote, reconnect: that is the downtime users feel.\n\nA check that always returns 200 is worse than no check. You will balance traffic onto a process that is not doing work."
  },
  "sticky-sessions": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Sticky sessions",
    "body": "**Sticky sessions pin a user to one instance, usually with a cookie the balancer sets**. You reach for it when session state lives in that process's memory.\n\nIt works until that instance dies. The user loses the session. It also unbalances load: one instance can hold a crowd while others sit idle, and you cannot scale in without logging those users out.\n\nThe alternative is a stateless app. Put the session in Redis or in a signed cookie that any instance can read. Then any server can take the next request, failover is a non-event, and autoscaling is safe.\n\nSticky is acceptable as a migration step, not as the design you defend. If you keep it, say what happens when the pinned instance disappears.\n\n**Sticky sessions are a migration step. A session any instance can read is the design.** *The pinned instance will die, and that user loses the cart that lived only in its memory.*\n\nThe balancer sets a cookie and sends the user back. It works until deploy, scale-in, or a crash. Load also skews: one instance holds a crowd. Put the session in Redis or in a signed cookie any process can verify. Then failover is a non-event and autoscaling can remove a node.\n\nIf you keep stickiness for a legacy in-memory session, say what the user sees when the node disappears: they log in again, or you rebuild from the database. You would not add more sticky rules for a new service. You would keep that service stateless from the start."
  },
  "connection-draining": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Connection draining",
    "body": "**Draining is how you remove an instance without cutting requests that are already running**. The balancer stops sending new work. In-flight requests finish, up to a timeout. Then the process exits.\n\nYou need it for deploys and scale-in. Without it, every deploy returns 502s for the requests that were on the old process.\n\nThe timeout has to be longer than your slowest honest request and shorter than the deploy budget. A request stuck for ten minutes will be killed. Long work belongs on a queue, not in the drain window.\n\nConsumers drain differently. They stop taking new messages, finish the current one, and commit the offset before exit. A kill during processing is a redelivery, which is why the handler is idempotent.\n\nKubernetes does this with a readiness flip and a `preStop` delay before the SIGTERM. The idea is the same outside Kubernetes.\n\n**Stop new work, finish what is in flight, then exit.** *The drain timeout has to cover an honest request and has to be shorter than the deploy. Long work belongs on a queue.*\n\nWithout draining, every deploy cuts the requests that were on the old process and the client sees 502. The balancer marks the instance out, waits, then the process gets SIGTERM. A request stuck for ten minutes will be killed. That is a bug in the request, not a reason to drain for ten minutes.\n\nConsumers drain by stopping the poll, finishing the current record, and committing the offset. A kill before that is a redelivery, which is safe only if the handler is idempotent. Kubernetes does the same idea with a readiness flip and a `preStop` delay. The name of the platform is not the point."
  },
  "api-gateway": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "API gateway",
    "body": "**A gateway is the front door for clients**. It terminates TLS, authenticates, rate-limits, and routes to services. The services do not each reimplement that.\n\nYou put it in when more than one client type exists or more than one service should not be exposed directly. You skip a separate gateway when one service is the whole product. A load balancer already covers TLS and routing.\n\nKeep business rules out of the gateway. Auth and a coarse rate limit belong there. \"Is this user allowed to refund this order\" belongs in the service that owns the order. A gateway that calls five services to assemble a page is a bottleneck and a distributed monolith.\n\nIt is a single point of failure if you run one instance. Run it as a fleet behind a balancer, with a config you can roll back. A bad route deploy should not require a code change in every service.\n\nWatch gateway latency separately from service latency. If the gateway adds 100 ms, the mesh of filters is the bug.\n\n**The gateway authenticates, rate-limits, and routes. The service that owns the row authorizes the action.** *A gateway that calls five services to build a page is a bottleneck and a distributed monolith.*\n\nMore than one client or more than one service that should not be exposed directly is the reason it exists. One service that is the whole product can live behind a load balancer and skip the extra hop. Keep refund rules out of the gateway. It would have to understand every resource, and every rule change would wait there.\n\nRun it as a fleet. One instance is a single point of failure. A bad route should roll back without a code change in every service. Watch gateway latency on its own. If it adds 100 ms, the filter chain is the bug, not the service behind it."
  },
  "cap-pacelc": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "CAP and PACELC",
    "body": "**CAP is about a partition, not about ordinary operation**. When a network split stops nodes from talking, you either refuse writes that might diverge (consistency) or accept them and repair later (availability). You cannot have both while the split lasts. Partition tolerance is not optional on a real network. The choice is C or A during the failure.\n\nPACELC adds the other half. If there is a partition (PA), you choose C or A. Else (ELC), even when the network is fine, you choose latency or consistency. A quorum read pays a round trip for a fresher answer. A local read is faster and can be stale.\n\nUse this when someone says \"we need strong consistency and five-nines and single-digit latency across regions.\" Those three do not coexist. Ask which one yields.\n\n*Do not cite CAP as a reason to skip transactions inside one database*. One primary is not a partition story. CAP starts when two replicas can both accept writes they cannot show each other.\n\n**During a partition you pick consistency or availability. While the network is fine you still pick latency or consistency.** *CAP is not a reason to skip a transaction inside one database.*\n\nA single primary is not a partition story. CAP starts when two replicas can both accept writes they cannot show each other. PACELC is the sentence that covers the ordinary day: a quorum read waits for a second copy and is fresher; a local read is faster and can be stale. Someone who asks for strong consistency, five-nines, and single-digit latency across regions is asking for three things that do not coexist. Ask which one yields.\n\nYou would not cite CAP and then refuse a local transaction on the order row. That row is one database. The trade-off starts when the second region can write."
  },
  "strong-vs-eventual": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Strong vs eventual consistency",
    "body": "**Strong consistency means a read after a committed write sees that write**. Users expect it for a balance, a seat they just reserved, and a permission you just revoked.\n\nEventual consistency means if writes stop, replicas will agree. They may not agree yet. Search indexes, feeds, notification badges, and a replica used for reports are eventual. The product has to tolerate the window.\n\nYou choose strong where a wrong read causes a wrong action: selling the last seat twice, showing a revoked admin token as valid. You choose eventual where the alternative is a slower, more coupled system and the user can refresh.\n\n\"Eventual\" is not a strategy by itself. Say how long, and what closes the gap: read-your-writes to the primary, a version check, or a repair job. A replica that is eventual by 100 ms is a different design from a feed that lags by an hour.\n\n**A read after a commit sees that commit, or it might not yet. Say which, and say how long.** *\"Eventual\" without a window is not a design.*\n\nA balance, a seat just reserved, and a permission just revoked need the strong read. A search index, a feed, a badge, and a report on a replica can lag. The product has to tolerate that lag. Close it on purpose: send the writer to the primary for their next read, check a version, or run a repair job. A replica that is 100 ms behind is a different system from a feed that lags an hour.\n\nYou would not call the cache strongly consistent, and you would not make every screen wait for a quorum because one of them is a badge."
  },
  "replication-quorum": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Replication and quorum",
    "body": "**A leader accepts writes and ships a log to followers**. Readers can use the leader and see the latest write, or a follower and maybe miss the last ones.\n\nA quorum is the minimum number of copies that must agree. With three replicas, a write quorum of two and a read quorum of two means the read overlaps the write, so you see the latest value. You pay two round trips. If a copy is down, you can still proceed. If two are down, you stop.\n\nYou use a leader when you want a simple order of writes: a database primary, a Kafka partition. You use a quorum system when there should be no single leader, or when you want reads and writes to survive a minority failure. Raft is the usual way a leader is elected so two nodes don't both think they are in charge.\n\nThe failure is a stale follower promoted too quickly, or a client still talking to the old leader. Fencing, a term number, or an epoch rejects the old leader's writes.\n\n**A write quorum and a read quorum that overlap see the latest value. You pay the extra round trips for that overlap.** *Two copies down, out of three, and you stop. That is the consistent choice.*\n\nA leader plus a log is the simple order: a database primary, a Kafka partition. A quorum system is for when you do not want a single leader, or you want to survive a minority failure. Raft is how a leader gets elected so two nodes do not both think they are in charge. With three replicas, write 2 and read 2 overlap. If you read from one follower, you might miss the last commit.\n\nThe failure is a stale follower promoted too fast, or a client still talking to the old leader. An epoch or a fencing token rejects the old leader's writes. You would not skip the fence because failover \"usually\" works."
  },
  "consensus": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Consensus",
    "body": "**Consensus is how a set of nodes agree on one value or one log when some of them are slow or dead**. Raft is the algorithm you should be able to sketch. Nodes elect a leader. The leader appends to a log. An entry is committed when a majority has it. A new leader must contain all committed entries.\n\nYou meet it inside systems you operate: Kafka controller, etcd under Kubernetes, a database primary election. You rarely implement it. You do need to know what it costs. A majority across regions means every write waits for a distant node. A majority in one region means a region loss can stall elections or lose the promise you thought you had.\n\nConsensus is not \"every node is identical and always available.\" A majority must be reachable. Split the five nodes evenly across two sites and a partition can leave both sides without a majority. That is a choice of availability versus a split brain, and the safe choice is to stop.\n\n**A majority must agree before a write is committed, and a new leader must contain every committed entry.** *You rarely implement Raft. You do have to know what a majority across regions costs.*\n\nThe leader appends. An entry is committed when a majority has it. You meet this inside systems you operate: a Kafka controller, etcd, a database election. A majority that includes another region means every write waits on that region. A majority that lives in one region means a region loss can stall elections or break the promise you thought you had. Five nodes split evenly across two sites can leave both sides without a majority. The safe result is that both sides stop.\n\nYou would not say \"every node is identical and always available.\" A majority has to be reachable, or the system refuses the write."
  },
  "sharding": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Sharding",
    "body": "**Sharding splits data across servers so writes and storage scale past one machine**. The shard key decides the split. The application or a proxy routes by that key.\n\nChoose the key from how you read. If every screen loads one customer, shard by customer. Scatter-gather, a query to every shard, is the tax you pay for a report that is not keyed. Keep those rare.\n\nResharding is the operation you will fear. Doubling shards means moving half the rows. Consistent hashing or a directory of key ranges makes that possible. It is still a data copy while traffic is live. Plan it before the disk is 95% full.\n\nCross-shard transactions are how a sharded system becomes slower than the single database you left. If two rows must commit together, put them on the same shard or split the business action into a saga.\n\nOne hot key still burns one shard. Sharding does not spread a single celebrity.\n\n**Shard by the key you read, and keep rows that must commit together on that same shard.** *A query that is not keyed is a scatter to every shard. Keep those rare.*\n\nIf every screen loads one customer, the key is the customer. A report across customers is the tax. Resharding is the operation you plan before the disk is 95% full: doubling shards moves half the rows while traffic is live. A directory of ranges or consistent hashing makes it possible. It is still a copy under load.\n\nOne hot key still burns one shard. Sharding does not spread a celebrity. Cross-shard transactions are how the new system becomes slower than the single database you left. If two rows must commit together, put them together or split the action into a saga, and say which before you draw the second box."
  },
  "partitions-split-brain": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Partitions and split brain",
    "body": "**A network partition means some nodes cannot talk to others, while both sides are still running**. Clients may reach both sides. If both sides accept writes, you have a split brain: two histories, and no honest way to merge a counter or a seat sale.\n\nThe guard is a majority. The side that cannot see a quorum stops accepting writes. The side with the quorum continues. Users of the stopped side see errors. That is the consistent choice. The available choice is both sides write, and you reconcile with lost updates or a manual merge.\n\nDatabases do this with a primary and fencing. Kafka does it with a partition leader. A human doing failover by promoting a replica while the old primary is still alive, but unreachable, is the classic split brain. STONITH or a fencing token exists so the old primary cannot keep writing.\n\nWhen you draw multi-region, say what happens when the link between regions dies. If you have not decided, the system will decide for you during the incident.\n\n**The side that cannot see a majority stops writing. The side with the majority continues.** *Both sides accepting writes is two histories, and a counter or a seat sale does not merge honestly.*\n\nClients may still reach both sides of a cut. The available choice is that both write and you reconcile later, with lost updates. The consistent choice is errors for the side that stopped. Databases do this with a primary and fencing. Kafka does it with a partition leader. The classic split brain is a human promoting a replica while the old primary is alive but unreachable. A fencing token exists so the old primary cannot keep writing.\n\nWhen you draw two regions, say what happens when the link dies. If you have not decided, the incident will decide for you."
  },
  "clocks": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Clocks",
    "body": "**Wall clocks on different machines are not the same clock**. NTP keeps them close and they still jump. You cannot use `timestamp > lastTimestamp` on two servers to order events, and you cannot use a local clock as a unique id.\n\nFor order, use a single leader's log position, a database sequence, or a logical clock that moves only when an event is recorded. For ids, use a random UUID or a scheme with a node id and a counter, not the time alone.\n\nTrueTime-style bounds exist in some systems so a commit waits out the uncertainty. You will not build that. You will avoid depending on cross-machine wall time for correctness.\n\nExpiry and TTLs are fine on one machine's monotonic clock. A lease that expires \"at 12:00:01 UTC\" compared across two hosts is how two processes both believe they hold the lock.\n\n**Do not order events from two machines by wall clock.** *NTP keeps clocks close. They still jump, and a lease that expires at \"12:00:01 UTC\" on two hosts is how both believe they hold the lock.*\n\nUse a single leader's log position, a database sequence, or a logical clock that moves when the event is recorded. For ids, use a random UUID or a node id plus a counter, not the time alone. TrueTime-style bounds exist so a commit waits out the uncertainty. You will not build that. You will avoid cross-machine wall time for correctness.\n\nExpiry on one process can use a monotonic clock. Comparing those instants across hosts is the bug. You would not use `timestamp > lastTimestamp` from two API servers to decide which write won."
  },
  "delivery-semantics": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Exactly-once, at-least-once, at-most-once",
    "body": "**These words describe a boundary**. Name the boundary or they are meaningless.\n\nAt-most-once across a queue: you may drop a message. You get it by committing before processing, or by not retrying. Use it for metrics you can lose.\n\nAt-least-once: you retry, so you may double. This is the default you should design for between services. The receiver deduplicates.\n\nExactly-once inside Kafka, from a consumer back to a Kafka topic, is a real transactional feature. Exactly-once from Kafka into a payment API is not. The API does not share Kafka's transaction. You store an idempotency key and accept that the effect happens once from the user's point of view, which is at-least-once plus dedup.\n\nIf the interviewer asks for exactly-once email, say you will send at least once and the provider will collapse duplicates on a message id. Promising the SMTP call happens once is a lie.\n\n**Name the boundary or the word is meaningless.** *Exactly-once from Kafka into a payment API is not a feature you can turn on. The API does not share Kafka's transaction.*\n\nAt-most-once is commit-before-work, or no retry. Metrics you can lose fit. At-least-once is the default between services, and the receiver deduplicates. Exactly-once inside Kafka, consumer back to a topic, is real. Exactly-once email is \"send at least once and collapse duplicates on a message id.\" Promising the SMTP call happens once is a lie.\n\nYou would not answer \"exactly-once\" and stop. Say where the id is stored, and what happens when the ack is lost after the effect succeeded."
  },
  "distributed-locks": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Distributed locks",
    "body": "**A distributed lock is a lease in a shared store, usually Redis or a database row, so two processes don't do the same job**. Redlock and a database `SELECT FOR UPDATE` are the variants people name.\n\nUse one when the work must not overlap: a scheduled payout, a shard of a migration. Do not use one as your default way to coordinate a request path. A lock held across a slow call becomes the deadlock you used to have inside one database, now with a network.\n\nThe lease must expire. A process that dies holding a lock without expiry stalls the system. Expiry means the work can still be running when someone else acquires the lock. The work itself has to be safe to overlap, or you fence with a token that the database rejects when the lease is old.\n\nA lock is not idempotency. Idempotency makes the second run harmless. A lock tries to stop the second run. Prefer idempotency when you can, because the lock will expire at the worst time.\n\n**A lock is a lease so two jobs do not overlap. Prefer idempotency when the second run can be harmless.** *A lease expires. The work can still be running when someone else acquires it.*\n\nUse a lock for a scheduled payout or a shard of a migration. Do not use one as the default way to coordinate a request path. A lock held across a slow call is the deadlock you used to have in one database, now with a network. The lease must expire or a dead holder stalls everyone. Because it expires, the work itself has to be safe to overlap, or you fence with a token the database rejects when the lease is old.\n\nRedlock and `SELECT FOR UPDATE` are the variants people name. Neither is a substitute for an idempotency key on a charge."
  },
  "cqrs": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "CQRS",
    "body": "**CQRS splits the model you write from the model you read**. Commands change state and enforce rules. Queries hit a shape built for the screen, often a different table or a search index.\n\nYou do it when the read pattern and the write pattern fight. An order is written as normalized rows. The support screen wants one document with customer, items, and shipment. Maintaining that document on write, or by consuming events, keeps the write model honest and the read fast.\n\n*You do not do it for a CRUD form where the row you write is the row you read*. Two models mean two places to change and a lag between them.\n\nThe read side is eventual. After a command succeeds, a query might not show it yet. If the user must see their own write, read from the write model for that request or wait for the projection.\n\nThe failure is a projection that crashes and rebuilds wrong. Keep the event or the write model so you can replay.\n\n**Split the write model from the read model when they fight. Do not split a form that reads the row it writes.** *The read side lags. After a command succeeds, the query might not show it yet.*\n\nAn order is stored as normalized rows. The support screen wants one document with customer, items, and shipment. Build that document on write, or from events. The write model stays honest and the screen stays fast. Two models mean two places to change. If the user must see their own write, read the write model for that request or wait for the projection.\n\nA projection that crashes and rebuilds wrong is the failure. Keep the events or the write model so you can replay. You would not add CQRS so the diagram looks advanced on a CRUD profile."
  },
  "event-sourcing": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Event sourcing",
    "body": "**Event sourcing stores the history of facts, not the latest row**. The current state is a fold of the events. You can rebuild a projection, audit why a balance is what it is, and add a new projection later.\n\nYou pay for that with complexity. Schema changes are new event versions you must still fold. A bug in the fold is a bug in every rebuild. Snapshots exist so you don't replay ten years to show one account.\n\nUse it when the history is the business: ledgers, workflows, cases where \"what happened\" is the question. Do not use it for a profile page. A row with `updated_at` is enough, and an audit table covers the rare investigation.\n\nIt pairs with CQRS because the events are the write side and the screens are projections. It does not remove the need for idempotency. A consumer that appends the same event twice doubles the balance unless the event id is unique.\n\n**Store the facts, and fold them into the current state, only when the history is the business.** *A bug in the fold is a bug in every rebuild. A profile page does not need this.*\n\nLedgers and workflows fit, because \"what happened\" is the question. A row with `updated_at` is enough for a profile, and an audit table covers the rare investigation. New event versions must still fold. Snapshots exist so you do not replay ten years to show one account. It pairs with CQRS: events are the write side, screens are projections.\n\nIt does not remove idempotency. A consumer that appends the same event twice doubles the balance unless the event id is unique. You would not event-source the user table because you might want an audit later."
  },
  "timeouts": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Timeouts",
    "body": "**Every call to another process needs a timeout**. Without one, a slow dependency holds your threads until the process is full of waiters and you are down too.\n\nSet it from the caller's budget, not from hope. If the user will wait 1 second, a downstream call cannot be allowed 30. Leave time for a fallback. A chain of timeouts must shrink as you go deeper, or the outer call gives up while the inner one is still running and still consuming a connection.\n\nA timeout does not mean the work failed. The payment may have been taken. The write may have committed. Treat timeout as unknown, and look up the result with an idempotency key before you retry the side effect.\n\nConnect timeout and read timeout are different. A connect timeout that is long just delays the discovery that the host is dead. A read timeout that is shorter than the real work will lie and create duplicates when you retry.\n\n**Every call to another process has a timeout, taken from the caller's budget.** *A timeout means unknown, not failed. Look the work up before you repeat a side effect.*\n\nIf the user will wait one second, the downstream call cannot be allowed thirty. Leave time for a fallback. A chain of timeouts must shrink as you go deeper, or the outer call gives up while the inner one still holds a connection. Connect timeout and read timeout are different. A long connect timeout only delays the news that the host is dead. A read timeout shorter than the real work lies, and the retry creates a duplicate.\n\nYou would not retry a payment on timeout with a new idempotency key. The first call may have committed. The key you stored before the call is how you find out."
  },
  "retries-backoff": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Retries, backoff, and jitter",
    "body": "**Retry when the failure is likely to disappear: a connection reset, a 503, a timeout to a dependency that is restarting**. Do not retry a 400 or a business rejection. You will get the same answer and add load.\n\nRetry only operations that are safe to repeat, or that carry an idempotency key. A retried POST /charges without a key is how you double-charge.\n\nBackoff spreads the attempts. Immediate retries arrive while the dependency is still dead and turn one outage into a storm. Exponential backoff waits 100 ms, then 200, then 400, with a cap. Jitter adds randomness so a thousand callers don't retry on the same millisecond and recreate the spike.\n\nCap the number of attempts. A retry budget shared across the process is better than each request retrying three times, because three times a thousand requests is the storm. When the budget is spent, fail the request and shed load.\n\n**Retry what is likely to disappear, and only when repeating it is safe.** *A 400 and a business rejection will not change if you ask again. You only add load.*\n\nA connection reset, a 503, a timeout to a process that is restarting: back off. 100 ms, then 200, then 400, with a cap, plus jitter so a thousand callers do not land on the same millisecond. Cap the attempts. A budget shared by the process is better than each request retrying three times, because three times a thousand requests is the storm. When the budget is spent, fail and shed.\n\nA retried `POST /charges` without an idempotency key is a double charge. If you cannot make the operation safe to repeat, do not automatically retry it."
  },
  "circuit-breaker": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Circuit breaker",
    "body": "**A circuit breaker stops calling a dependency that is failing**. Closed is normal. After enough failures, it opens and calls fail immediately, without waiting for the timeout. After a cool-down it half-opens and lets a few calls through. Success closes it. Failure opens it again.\n\nYou use it when a dependency is slow rather than merely down. Timeouts alone still occupy a thread for the whole timeout. An open breaker fails in microseconds and gives the dependency room to recover. You pair it with a fallback: a cached value, a smaller feature, or a clear error.\n\n*You do not put a breaker on a local function or on a database write that must not be skipped*. Skipping a charge is not a fallback.\n\nThe breaker is per dependency, sometimes per endpoint. One giant breaker opens because a minor route failed and you then refuse the healthy routes. Watch open/close events. A breaker that flaps means the threshold is too sensitive or the dependency is right on the edge.\n\n**When a dependency is failing, stop waiting for it.** *An open breaker fails in microseconds. A timeout still occupies a thread for the whole wait.*\n\nClosed is normal. Enough failures open it. After a cool-down, a few calls try again. Success closes it. Pair it with a fallback you can name: a cached value, a smaller feature, or a clear error. Do not put a breaker on a charge and then \"fall back\" by skipping the charge.\n\nOne breaker per dependency, sometimes per route. One giant breaker opens because a minor route failed and then you refuse the healthy routes. A breaker that flaps is a threshold that is too sensitive, or a dependency sitting on the edge. Watch the open and close events. You would not add a breaker and no timeout. The calls that slip through while it is closed still need a deadline."
  },
  "bulkhead": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Bulkhead",
    "body": "**A bulkhead isolates resources so one dependency cannot consume them all**. Separate thread pools or connection pools for payments and for recommendations. When recommendations hang, they fill their own pool. Payments still have threads.\n\nYou use it where a slow optional call sits next to a required one. You also use it between tenants if one tenant's batch job would otherwise take the whole pool.\n\nThe size of the pool is the limit. Too small and you throttle yourself on a healthy day. Too large and you are back to sharing one pool. Size it from the concurrency you can afford, and queue or reject past that.\n\nA bulkhead without a timeout still fills. The hung calls sit in the isolated pool until it is exhausted, and then that feature fails. That is the point. The rest of the process survives. Combine it with a timeout so the pool drains.\n\n**Give the optional call its own pool so it cannot spend the threads the payment call needs.** *A bulkhead without a timeout still fills. The hung calls sit until that pool is exhausted, and then that feature fails. The rest of the process should survive.*\n\nRecommendations and payments do not share a pool. One tenant's batch does not share the pool with interactive traffic. Size the pool from the concurrency you can afford. Too small and you throttle a healthy day. Too large and you are back to one shared pool. Past the limit, queue briefly or reject.\n\nYou would not isolate every method into its own pool. You isolate the dependency that can hang from the one that must not."
  },
  "rate-limiting": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Rate limiting",
    "body": "**A rate limit caps how fast a caller can hit you**. It protects the database and the downstream from a client bug, a retry storm, or abuse. The usual answer to an excess request is HTTP 429 with a `Retry-After`.\n\nDecide the key: API key, user, IP, or route. A limit on IP punishes NAT users who share an address. A limit on user misses unauthenticated floods. Edge limits and application limits can both exist. The edge stops volume. The application enforces a business quota.\n\nA token bucket allows a burst and then a steady rate. A sliding window stops the trick of spending two windows' quota in one second at the boundary. Either is fine if you can say what burst you allow.\n\nWhere the counter lives matters. In-memory is fast and per instance, so ten instances allow ten times the limit. Redis is shared and adds a hop. For abuse control, a factor of ten is often acceptable. For a hard billing quota, it is not.\n\nFail open or closed when the counter store is down, and say which. Payments fail closed. A public read API often fails open with a coarse local cap.\n\n**Cap a caller before their bug, their retries, or their abuse become your database's problem.** *Ten app instances with an in-memory counter allow about ten times the limit. That is fine for abuse and wrong for a hard billing quota.*\n\nThe answer to the excess request is 429 and `Retry-After`. Choose the key: API key, user, or IP. An IP limit punishes people behind one NAT. A user limit misses an unauthenticated flood. A token bucket allows a burst and then a steady rate. A sliding window stops the trick of spending two windows in the boundary second. Say which burst you allow.\n\nWhen the counter store is down, fail closed for payments and fail open, with a coarse local cap, for a public read. You would not hide the limit inside the service and also forget the edge. Volume and business quota are different limits."
  },
  "backpressure": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Backpressure and load shedding",
    "body": "**Backpressure is the downstream telling the upstream to slow down**. A bounded queue that blocks or rejects the producer is backpressure. An unbounded queue is a memory leak with a delay.\n\nLoad shedding is dropping work on purpose when you are past capacity. You shed the cheap or optional work first: recommendations, analytics beacons, anonymous browse traffic. You keep payments and health checks.\n\nYou need both when producers are faster than consumers. Kafka's lag is backpressure only if producers notice and slow down. If they keep publishing, the log grows until disk fills, and then everyone stops. A max lag or a full disk is a brutal form of backpressure.\n\nOn a request path, shedding is a 429 or 503 before you accept work you cannot finish. Accepting it and timing out still costs a thread and a connection.\n\nThe signal is queue depth, pool wait time, or p99 latency. Shedding on CPU alone can be late.\n\n**A bounded queue tells the producer to slow down. An unbounded queue is a memory leak with a delay.** *Accepting work you cannot finish still costs a thread. Shed before you accept it.*\n\nDrop the cheap work first: recommendations, analytics, anonymous browsing. Keep payments and health checks. Kafka lag is backpressure only if producers notice and slow down. If they keep publishing, the disk fills and everyone stops. On a request path, shedding is a 429 or a503 before the handler runs.\n\nThe signal is queue depth, pool wait, or p99, not CPU after you are already late. You would not add an infinite buffer \"so we never drop.\" You will drop later, in a worse place."
  },
  "graceful-degradation": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Graceful degradation",
    "body": "**Degradation is a smaller product that still works when a part is down**. The product page loads without recommendations. Checkout works without the loyalty-points call. Search falls back to the database for an exact id when the index is down, and refuses full-text rather than hanging.\n\nDecide the cuts before the incident. A feature flag or a fallback branch that you have tested is degradation. A stack trace the user sees is not.\n\nEach fallback has a cost. Falling back from cache to database is correct only if the database can take the load. Otherwise the fallback is the outage. Cap it.\n\nTell the user as much as you need to and no more. A banner that reviews are unavailable is enough. Retrying the dead dependency on every page view is how you make it worse.\n\nWrite down the order: what you turn off first, and what you never turn off.\n\n**Decide the cuts before the incident. A tested fallback is degradation. A stack trace is not.** *Falling back from cache to database is safe only if the database can take the load. Otherwise the fallback is the outage.*\n\nThe product page loads without recommendations. Checkout works without the loyalty call. Search can answer an exact id from the database and refuse full-text rather than hang. Cap that fallback. Tell the user with a banner, not a retry of the dead dependency on every view.\n\nWrite the order down: what you turn off first, and what you never turn off. You would not degrade payments so that recommendations can stay up."
  },
  "rto-rpo": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "RTO and RPO",
    "body": "**RPO is how much data you can lose**. RTO is how long you can be down. They are business numbers. You turn them into a design.\n\nAn RPO of zero means a committed write cannot vanish. That pushes you to synchronous replication or a write that is not acknowledged until a second copy has it. You pay latency.\n\nAn RPO of minutes means async replication is enough, and a crash can lose the tail. Say the tail out loud.\n\nAn RTO of minutes means a human cannot be in the failover path. Health checks, promotion, and client reconnect have to be automatic. An RTO of hours allows a restore from backup, which is a different and cheaper design.\n\nBackups set the RPO if replication is not the plan. A nightly snapshot is up to 24 hours of loss. Test the restore. A backup you have not restored is a hope.\n\nAsk for the two numbers before you draw a second region. Multi-region is how you buy a low RTO against a region loss, and it is expensive. Many systems need a low RTO for a single-instance failure and can wait hours for a region loss.\n\n**RPO is how much data you can lose. RTO is how long you can be down. Get the two numbers before you draw a second region.** *Zero loss means the ack waits for a second copy. Minutes of loss means async replication and a tail you name out loud.*\n\nAn RTO of minutes means a human is not in the failover path. An RTO of hours can be a restore from backup, which is cheaper. A nightly snapshot is up to 24 hours of loss. A backup you have not restored is a hope. Many systems need a low RTO for one instance and can wait hours for a region. Multi-region is how you buy the second, and it is expensive.\n\nYou would not promise RPO zero and RTO seconds and single-digit write latency across continents in the same sentence."
  },
  "active-active-passive": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Active-active vs active-passive",
    "body": "**Active-passive keeps a spare that does not take traffic until failover**. Writes go to one place. The spare is a replica. This is the simpler consistency story and the usual database setup. You waste the spare's capacity, or you use it for reads and accept staleness.\n\nActive-active sends traffic to more than one site at once. You use the capacity you pay for, and a site loss removes only part of the traffic. You now have conflict. Two sites can sell the last seat or update the same profile. You need a shard owner, a conflict rule, or a data type that merges, such as a counter with caveats.\n\nFor most principal designs, the app tier is active-active behind a balancer in one region, and the database is active-passive. A second region is a warm passive until the product truly needs to survive a region loss.\n\nIf you claim active-active writes, the interviewer will ask what happens when both regions update the same key. Have the answer.\n\n**The app tier can be active-active. The database, for most of these designs, is active-passive.** *If both regions accept writes to the same key, you need an owner, a merge rule, or a type that merges. \"Last write wins\" on a seat is a lost sale.*\n\nActive-passive keeps a spare that does not take writes until failover. Consistency is simpler. You waste the spare, or you read from it and accept lag. Active-active uses the capacity you pay for, and a site loss removes only part of the traffic. You now have conflict.\n\nA second region as a warm passive is the usual answer until the business will pay to survive a region loss. If you claim active-active writes, the next question is the same key in both regions. Have the answer before you draw the arrows."
  },
  "multi-az-region": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Multi-AZ vs multi-region",
    "body": "**An availability zone is a separate data center in the same region**. Latency between zones is low, a millisecond or two. Multi-AZ replication is how a database survives a building failure without giving up strong reads on the primary. Put instances in more than one zone so one zone's power cut does not take every copy.\n\nA region is a different geography. Latency is tens or hundreds of milliseconds. Synchronous writes across regions make every commit slow. Use async replication, and accept the RPO, or don't do multi-region writes.\n\nYou go multi-AZ by default for anything you mean to keep up. You go multi-region when a region loss is in the threat model the business will pay for. Many interviews are satisfied with multi-AZ plus a backup in another region for restore, which is not the same as serving traffic there.\n\nDNS or anycast decides which region a user hits. Failover at that layer is minutes unless you have rehearsed it. That time is part of the RTO.\n\n**More than one zone is the default for anything you mean to keep up. Another region is a product decision.** *Synchronous writes across regions make every commit slow. Use async replication and accept the RPO, or do not do multi-region writes.*\n\nZones in one region are a millisecond or two apart. A primary and a standby across zones survive a building without giving up a strong read on the primary. Regions are tens or hundreds of milliseconds apart. Serving traffic in the second region is not the same as storing a backup there for restore.\n\nDNS or anycast decides which region a user hits. Failover at that layer is minutes unless you have rehearsed it, and that time is part of the RTO. You would not draw two regions and call the link free."
  },
  "idempotency": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Idempotency",
    "body": "**An operation is idempotent when doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once**. GET and PUT of a full document are naturally close. POST /payments is not, unless you make it so.\n\nThe client sends `Idempotency-Key: abc123`. You store the key and the response. The first request runs. A retry with the same key returns the stored response and does not charge again. The key is unique per intended action, not per attempt.\n\n```\nPOST /payments\nIdempotency-Key: abc123\n```\n\nStore the key in the same transaction as the effect. A key written after the charge races with the retry and you charge twice. Keep keys for as long as clients retry, which is at least a day for payments.\n\nThe same idea on a consumer is `processed_event`. The event id is the key.\n\nRetries without idempotency are how a timeout becomes a second order. If you cannot make the operation idempotent, do not automatically retry it.\n\n**The key is unique per intended action, and it is stored in the same transaction as the effect.** *A key written after the charge races the retry, and you charge twice.*\n\nThe client sends `Idempotency-Key` on the first attempt and on every retry of that attempt. You store the key and the response. The second call returns the stored response and does not run the side effect again. A new key means a new action. Keep keys at least as long as clients retry, which for payments is at least a day. The same idea on a consumer is the event id in `processed_event`.\n\nIf you cannot make the operation safe to repeat, do not automatically retry it. A timeout is the case that matters: the work may have committed and the response may have died."
  },
  "outbox": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Outbox",
    "body": "**The outbox solves the dual write**. You must update the database and publish an event. If you publish and then crash before the commit, you emit a fact that never happened. If you commit and then crash before the publish, the fact happened and nobody hears it.\n\nWrite the business row and an outbox row in one transaction. A separate publisher reads the outbox and sends to Kafka. It marks the row published after the broker acks. A crash retries the send. Consumers must be idempotent because the send can happen twice.\n\n```\nOrder Service\n  |\nDatabase transaction\n  |-- order row\n  |-- outbox row\n        |\n     Publisher\n        |\n      Kafka\n```\n\nYou choose this whenever a committed change must leave the service. You reject \"publish then commit\" and \"commit then publish\" as two failure modes you have already hit in production somewhere.\n\nThe publisher has to be running. An outbox table that grows and is never read is a silent outage. Alert on the age of the oldest unpublished row.\n\n**The business row and the outbox row commit together. A publisher sends after that, and it may send twice.** *Publish-then-commit emits a fact that never happened. Commit-then-publish loses the fact if you crash in between.*\n\nThe publisher marks the row only after the broker acks. Consumers dedupe on the event id. You use this whenever a committed change must leave the service. The publisher has to be running. An outbox that grows and is never read is a silent outage. Alert on the age of the oldest unpublished row, not only on the count.\n\nYou would not \"also publish to Kafka in the request\" beside the outbox and hope one of the paths works. One path, the transactional one, is the design."
  },
  "saga": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Saga",
    "body": "**A saga is a business action split across services, each with a local transaction, plus a compensation if a later step fails**. There is no lock held across all of them.\n\nOrder, payment, inventory, shipping. The order is created. Payment is taken. Inventory cannot reserve the unit. You refund, and the order becomes cancelled. The refund is the compensation, not a database rollback. The charge really happened.\n\nOrchestration puts one coordinator in charge of the steps. It is easier to read and it is a thing that can be down. Choreography lets each service emit events and react. It is looser and harder to see the whole path.\n\nEvery step must be idempotent. The coordinator will retry. A compensation must be safe to run twice, and safe to run when the step never succeeded. That is the hard part, not the happy path.\n\nYou use a saga when you cannot put the data in one database. If you can, a local transaction is simpler and you should say so.\n\n**Each step is a local transaction. A later failure runs a compensation, not a rollback of someone else's database.** *The charge really happened. The refund is a new action with its own key.*\n\nOrder, then payment, then inventory. Inventory cannot reserve the unit, so you refund and cancel. Orchestration is one coordinator you can read and that can be down. Choreography is events and reactions, looser and harder to see. Every step, including the compensation, must be safe to run twice, and safe to run when the step never succeeded.\n\nIf the rows can live in one database, a local transaction is simpler and you should say so. A saga is what you use when they cannot."
  },
  "tcp": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "TCP",
    "body": "**TCP gives you a reliable, ordered byte stream**. The handshake is SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, before any HTTP. A new connection to a cold host costs that round trip plus TLS. Keep-alive exists so the next request skips it.\n\nReliability is retransmission. The sender keeps bytes until they are acknowledged. If the ack is late, it sends again. The receiver drops duplicates and reorders. You do not reimplement that in the application. You still handle duplicates at the business level, because a retried HTTP request is a second request, not a TCP duplicate.\n\nCongestion control slows the sender when packets are lost, on the theory that loss means the path is full. A chatty service across a lossy link gets a small window and looks \"slow\" with low CPU. More threads will not fix a window that is closed.\n\nWhen a call hangs, the interesting question is whether the handshake finished and which side is not acking. A connect timeout and a read timeout tell you different halves of that.\n\n**TCP gives you an ordered byte stream and retransmits. A retried HTTP request is still a second request.** *You do not reimplement retransmission in the application, and you still dedupe the business action.*\n\nThe handshake is a round trip before any HTTP, plus TLS on a cold connection. Keep-alive exists so the next call skips it. Congestion control shrinks the window when packets are lost. A chatty service on a lossy link looks slow with low CPU. More threads will not open a window that is closed.\n\nWhen a call hangs, ask whether the handshake finished and which side is not acking. Connect timeout and read timeout are the two halves. You would not raise the thread pool to fix a path that is waiting on acks."
  },
  "udp-vs-tcp": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "UDP vs TCP",
    "body": "**TCP is a connection, ordered and retransmitted**. UDP is datagrams. You get no handshake, no retransmission, and no order. You also get no head-of-line blocking: one lost packet does not stall the next.\n\nUse TCP for requests and responses where a missing byte is a bug: HTTP, database wire protocols, Kafka. Use UDP when late is worse than missing: voice, video, some games, DNS queries. QUIC, under HTTP/3, is UDP plus a reliability layer the application stack owns, so you get encryption and streams without TCP's one-loss-blocks-all behavior.\n\n*You do not pick UDP to \"go faster\" for an API*. You would rebuild TCP badly. You pick it when the product can drop a sample.\n\nFirewalls and NAT time out UDP mappings quickly. A long-lived UDP flow needs keepalive packets or it silently dies. TCP keepalives are a different, slower mechanism.\n\n**TCP when a missing byte is a bug. UDP when a late sample is worse than a missing one.** *You do not pick UDP to make a JSON API faster. You would rebuild TCP, badly.*\n\nHTTP, database protocols, and Kafka want TCP. Voice, video, some games, and DNS queries want UDP, because one lost packet must not stall the next. QUIC, under HTTP/3, is UDP plus a reliability layer, so one loss stalls one stream instead of every stream on the connection.\n\nNAT mappings for UDP die quickly. A long-lived flow needs keepalive packets or it silently disappears. You would not run an order API on raw UDP and then add your own acks."
  },
  "http-versions": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3",
    "body": "**HTTP/1**.1 is one request at a time per connection, unless you pipeline, which barely works. Browsers open several connections to fake parallelism. Head-of-line blocking means a slow response holds the connection.\n\nHTTP/2 multiplexes many streams on one TCP connection. Headers are compressed. You stop needing six connections per host. One lost TCP packet still stalls every stream on that connection, because TCP is one pipe.\n\nHTTP/3 runs those streams over QUIC on UDP. A lost packet stalls one stream, not the others. Connection setup is faster on a repeat visit. Middleboxes that expect TCP are the operational surprise.\n\nFor a service-to-service JSON API inside a data center, HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 over a pooled connection is enough. The upgrade matters at the edge, where latency and loss are real. gRPC wants HTTP/2. Don't promise HTTP/3 as the fix for a slow database.\n\n**HTTP/2 puts many streams on one TCP connection. One lost TCP packet still stalls all of them. HTTP/3 moves the streams onto QUIC so one loss stalls one stream.** *None of that fixes a slow database.*\n\nHTTP/1.1 is one request at a time per connection, so browsers open several. HTTP/2 compresses headers and multiplexes. gRPC wants HTTP/2. HTTP/3 setup is faster on a repeat visit, and middleboxes that expect TCP are the surprise. Inside a data center, HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 on a pooled connection is enough. The upgrade matters at the edge, where latency and loss are real.\n\nYou would not promise HTTP/3 as the remedy for a query that takes a second."
  },
  "tls": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "TLS",
    "body": "**TLS is the handshake and the encryption on top of the connection**. The client checks the server certificate against a name. That is how you know you reached the host you asked for, not a machine in the middle.\n\nThe handshake costs a round trip or two. Session resumption and keep-alive exist so you don't pay it on every request. Terminating TLS at the load balancer offloads the app and means traffic inside the VPC may be plain. Say whether that is acceptable. Between services, mTLS or a mesh often re-encrypts.\n\nCertificates expire. The outage mode is a clock or a renewal job, and every handshake fails at once. Alert on expiry weeks ahead.\n\nTLS does not make the API authenticated. It authenticates the server to the client, unless you also check client certificates. The user identity is still a token or a session on top.\n\n**TLS is how the client knows it reached the host it asked for, and how the bytes are encrypted.** *It does not authenticate the user. A token or a session still has to.*\n\nThe handshake is a round trip or two. Resumption and keep-alive exist so you do not pay it every time. Terminating TLS at the load balancer offloads the app and may leave the inside of the network plain. Say whether that is acceptable. Between services, mTLS or a mesh often encrypts again.\n\nCertificates expire together when a renewal job fails. Alert weeks ahead. You would not treat \"we have HTTPS\" as the answer to \"how do twenty services know the caller.\""
  },
  "dns": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "DNS",
    "body": "**DNS turns a name into an address before the TCP handshake**. A resolver asks, gets an A or AAAA record, and caches it for the TTL. Until that TTL, clients keep the old address.\n\nThat cache is why failover by changing a DNS record is slow. Some resolvers ignore low TTLs. A move that looks instant in the zone file still leaves users on the old IP. For a fast failover you want a load balancer that already has both targets, not a DNS change.\n\nUse DNS for stable names: the gateway, the database hostname that points at the current primary via a record you update carefully. Don't resolve once at process start and cache forever unless you refresh. A Java process that caches DNS indefinitely will talk to a dead IP after a failover.\n\nMultiple A records are a crude load spread at the client. Health is not part of DNS unless you added something that removes bad records. A record can point at a dead host until you change it.\n\n**A name becomes an address, and resolvers cache it for the TTL. Failover by changing a record is as slow as that cache, and some resolvers ignore a low TTL.** *For a fast failover, both targets already sit behind a balancer. You do not wait on DNS.*\n\nUse DNS for stable names: the gateway, the database hostname you update carefully. A process that resolves once at startup and caches forever will talk to a dead IP after a failover. Java has done this. Refresh. Multiple A records spread clients crudely and do not know which host is healthy unless something removes the bad record.\n\nYou would not set a TTL of 5 seconds and call the failover instant. Users will still be on the old address."
  },
  "connection-pooling": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Connection pooling and keep-alive",
    "body": "**A new TCP and TLS handshake per request dominates short calls**. Keep-alive leaves the connection open and the next request reuses it. A pool is the set of those connections a process will keep.\n\nAt the database, the pool size is the number of concurrent queries one instance will run, not the number of user requests. A request holds a connection only while the query runs. Size the pool so the database's max connections are above `instances × pool`, with headroom for admin. A pool of 100 on 50 instances is 5,000 connections and a database that spends its time scheduling them.\n\nHTTP clients pool too. A pool that is smaller than the concurrency blocks threads waiting for a socket, which looks like a slow dependency. A pool that is huge opens more sockets than the server will accept.\n\nIdle connections get killed by firewalls. The pool has to detect a dead socket and open a new one, or the first request after a quiet period fails. Validate on borrow, or set a max idle age below the firewall's timeout.\n\n**A pool is how many connections this process will keep, not how many users you have.** *`instances × pool` has to fit under the database's max connections, with room for admin.*\n\nA new TCP and TLS handshake dominates a short call, so keep-alive reuses the socket. A request holds a database connection only while the query runs. A pool of 100 on 50 instances is 5,000 connections, and the database spends its time scheduling them. An HTTP pool that is smaller than the concurrency blocks threads waiting for a socket, which looks like a slow dependency. A pool that is huge gets refused.\n\nIdle sockets die at firewalls. Detect a dead connection or the first request after a quiet period fails. You would not size the pool to the number of threads \"to be safe.\""
  },
  "websockets-sse": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "WebSockets vs SSE",
    "body": "**Server-Sent Events are a one-way stream over ordinary HTTP**. The server pushes text events. The client sends new work with a normal POST. Use SSE for a live feed, a job log, or notifications where the client does not need to send on the same pipe.\n\nWebSockets are bidirectional and stay open. Use them for chat, presence, and collaborative editing. They are not HTTP after the upgrade, so some proxies and auth filters stop applying unless they are written for the upgrade.\n\nBoth hold a connection per client. A million users is a million sockets, which is a fleet and a file-descriptor limit, not a function call. The process that holds the socket is stateful. Another instance does not have that user. Publish the event on a bus and let each gateway push to the sockets it owns.\n\nReconnects happen. The client needs the last event id and the server needs to resume, or the client reloads history. A socket is not a durable queue.\n\n**SSE is a one-way stream over ordinary HTTP. WebSockets are bidirectional and stop being HTTP after the upgrade.** *The process that holds the socket is stateful. Another instance does not have that user.*\n\nA live log or a notification feed fits SSE. The client posts new work with a normal request. Chat and presence fit WebSockets. Proxies and auth filters that do not understand the upgrade will break it. A million users is a million sockets, a fleet, and a file-descriptor limit. Publish the event on a bus and let each gateway push to the sockets it owns.\n\nClients reconnect. They need the last event id, or they reload history. A socket is not a durable queue. You would not keep the only copy of a chat message in the gateway's memory."
  },
  "rest-vs-grpc": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "REST vs gRPC",
    "body": "**REST over HTTP and JSON is what browsers and third parties already speak**. It is easy to debug with a curl log. Contracts are looser unless you add OpenAPI and actually check it. Use it at the edge.\n\ngRPC is HTTP/2 plus protobuf. The contract is a `.proto` file. You get generated clients, smaller payloads, and streams. Use it between your own services when you want a hard contract and the latency of JSON parsing matters. You give up casual curl and browser support unless you add a JSON gateway.\n\nProtobuf schemas evolve by adding optional fields, not by renumbering tags. That is the backward-compatibility rule. A required field you add later breaks old clients.\n\n*Don't rewrite a public API as gRPC for speed*. The database is almost certainly the cost. Do use a schema, REST or proto, so a producer cannot silently change a type.\n\n**JSON at the edge, where browsers and other companies already speak it. A hard contract between your own services when you want one.** *The database is almost always the cost. Rewriting a public API as gRPC will not move it.*\n\nREST is easy to debug and loose unless you check an OpenAPI document. gRPC is HTTP/2 and protobuf: generated clients, smaller payloads, streams, and no casual curl unless you add a JSON gateway. Add optional fields. Do not renumber tags or change a type. A required field you add later breaks old clients.\n\nYou would not skip a schema on either side. A producer that silently changes a type is the outage, whether the bytes were JSON or protobuf."
  },
  "reverse-proxy": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Reverse proxy",
    "body": "**A reverse proxy sits in front of your servers and accepts the client connection**. The client thinks the proxy is the site. Nginx, HAProxy, Envoy, and the cloud load balancers all play this role.\n\nIt terminates TLS, routes by host or path, buffers slow clients so your app sees a complete request, and can cache. A forward proxy is the opposite: it sits in front of clients and reaches out. Don't mix the two up in the interview.\n\nThe proxy is part of the timeout story. It has an idle timeout. If it is shorter than a long poll or an upload, it cuts the connection and the app may still be working. Align them.\n\nIt also hides instance IPs, which is what you want. Health checks on the proxy decide who is in the upstream. A misconfigured proxy that points at one instance is a load balancer that does not balance.\n\nPut compression and coarse rate limits here when they are the same for every service. Put business auth next to the business when the proxy would have to understand every rule.\n\n**The proxy accepts the client connection and hides the instances.** *Its idle timeout cuts a long poll or an upload if it is shorter than the work, and the app may still be running.*\n\nNginx, HAProxy, Envoy, and the cloud load balancers terminate TLS, route by host or path, and buffer a slow client. A forward proxy sits in front of clients. Do not mix them up. Health checks on the proxy decide who is upstream. A proxy pointed at one instance is a balancer that does not balance.\n\nPut compression and a coarse rate limit here when they are the same for every service. Put \"may this user refund this order\" next to the order. You would not debug a 502 without asking which timeout fired, the proxy's or the app's."
  },
  "request-path": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "What happens when a request hits the system",
    "body": "**Walk it in order, and stop at the first place it can fail**.\n\nThe client resolves DNS and gets an IP. That IP is usually the load balancer, not an app server. TCP connects, then TLS. A failure here is a name, a certificate, or a security group. The request has not reached your code.\n\nThe balancer picks an instance that is healthy and forwards. L7 can route on the path. A failure here is no healthy targets.\n\nThe app reads headers, authenticates, checks a rate limit, and does the work. That work is a cache lookup and a database query, or a publish. A timeout here is your pool or the dependency.\n\nNAT and firewalls matter at the boundaries. A service in a private subnet needs a route and a security group to reach the database. \"It works on my laptop\" often dies at this hop. The OSI labels are only useful as a way to say which hop: name, connection, TLS, HTTP, application. You do not need to recite all seven layers.\n\nSSH is how you debug a box. It is not part of the request path.\n\n**Walk the hops in order and stop at the first one that can fail.** *The IP from DNS is usually the load balancer, not your process. A failure before that is a name, a certificate, or a security group. Your code has not run.*\n\nThen the balancer picks a healthy instance. No healthy targets is a failure there. Then the app authenticates, limits, and does a cache lookup, a query, or a publish. A timeout there is the pool or the dependency. A private subnet needs a route and a security group to reach the database. \"It works on my laptop\" often dies at that hop.\n\nThe useful labels are name, connection, TLS, HTTP, and application. You do not recite seven layers. SSH is how you debug a box. It is not on the path."
  },
  "api-resources-status": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Resources and status codes",
    "body": "**A REST resource is a noun the client can talk about: `/orders/42`, not `/createOrder`**. The method is the verb. GET reads and must not change state. POST creates or starts an action whose id the server chooses. PUT replaces. PATCH edits. DELETE removes. Clients and caches rely on GET being safe.\n\nStatus codes are the contract more than the JSON body. 200 with an error field teaches every client to parse prose. Use 201 when you created, 202 when you accepted work for later, 400 for a bad payload, 401 when you don't know the caller, 403 when you know them and they may not, 404 when the resource is not theirs or is absent, 409 for a conflict such as a version mismatch, 429 when they are over the limit, 503 when you are.\n\nIdempotent methods can be retried by a proxy. POST cannot, unless you documented an idempotency key. Say that when you pick the method.\n\nErrors should be a stable code plus a message. Don't make the client match English.\n\n**The resource is a noun. The method is the verb. The status code is the contract.** *200 with an error field in the JSON teaches every client to parse prose.*\n\n`/orders/42`, not `/createOrder`. GET must not change state, because clients and caches retry it. 201 created, 202 accepted for later, 400 bad payload, 401 unknown caller, 403 known and refused, 404 absent or not theirs, 409 a version conflict, 429 over the limit, 503 you are unavailable. POST is not safe to retry unless you documented an idempotency key.\n\nErrors are a stable code plus a message. You would not make the client match an English sentence, and you would not return 200 because the load balancer only alerts on 500."
  },
  "pagination": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Pagination and filtering",
    "body": "**Offset pagination (`**?page=10&size=50`) is easy and wrong at the tail of a large table. The database still walks the first 500 rows, and a new row inserted on page one shifts everything so the client skips or repeats an item.\n\nCursor pagination returns a stable token, often the last id or `(created_at, id)`. The next query is `WHERE (created_at, id) < cursor ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 50`. It uses an index and does not drift. You cannot jump to page 40. Most feeds don't need to.\n\nFilters and sorts belong in query parameters and must match an index. A free-form sort on an unindexed column is a scan you offered to the internet. Cap the page size. A client that asks for a million rows is a report, and reports belong on a replica or an export job.\n\nSay what is stable. If the sort field is not unique, include a unique tie-break or two rows with the same timestamp will appear twice or vanish.\n\n**A cursor beats an offset once the table is large.** *Offset still walks the skipped rows, and an insert on page one shifts the next page so the client skips or repeats an item.*\n\nThe token is the last `(created_at, id)`. The next query is the rows before that pair, limited, using the index. You cannot jump to page 40. Most feeds do not need to. Filters and sorts are query parameters and must match an index. A free-form sort on an unindexed column is a scan you offered to the internet. Cap the page size. A million rows is a report, and reports run on a replica or an export.\n\nIf the sort field is not unique, add a unique tie-break or two rows with the same timestamp appear twice or vanish."
  },
  "api-versioning": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Versioning and backward compatibility",
    "body": "**You change an API without breaking old clients by adding, not by changing meaning**. A new optional JSON field is safe. Renaming a field, changing a type, or making a formerly optional field required is a break.\n\nPrefer compatibility inside a version for as long as you can. When you truly must break, put a new resource or a new path (`/v2/orders`) and run both until the old clients are gone. Header versioning works and is easier to miss in a log. Path versioning is obvious. Either is fine if you can route and observe them separately.\n\nA consumer of events has the same rule. Add a field. Don't reuse a tag or a name to mean something else. Protobuf field numbers are forever.\n\nHave a removal date and a metric of who still calls the old path. An old version with no sunset is how you end up with three checkouts. A sunset with no metric is how you turn off a client you forgot.\n\n**Add a field. Do not change what an old field means.** *A rename, a type change, or a newly required field is a break, whether you called it a version or not.*\n\nStay compatible inside a version for as long as you can. When you must break, run `/v2/orders` beside the old path until the old clients are gone. A header version works and is easier to miss in a log. A path version is obvious. Either is fine if you can route and count them separately. Event consumers follow the same rule. Protobuf field numbers are forever.\n\nHave a date and a metric of who still calls the old path. A sunset with no metric turns off a client you forgot. An old version with no sunset becomes a third checkout."
  },
  "webhooks": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Webhooks",
    "body": "**A webhook is the client's HTTP endpoint that you call when something happens**. It inverts the usual flow. You are the client, and their server must be up.\n\nSign the body with a secret so they can tell the call is yours. Include an event id so they can dedupe. Retry with backoff when they return 5xx or time out. Give up and surface the failure after a bounded set of attempts. A webhook that retries forever on a 404 is an outage you cause yourself.\n\nTimeouts are unknown. They may have processed the event and failed to respond. The event id is what makes the retry safe.\n\n*Don't put the only copy of the event in the webhook call*. Persist it, then deliver, the same way an outbox works. If their endpoint is slow, it must not hold a thread per pending event. A queue of deliveries is the design.\n\nLet them rotate the secret and see recent deliveries. You will debug this with them on a call.\n\n**Persist the event, then deliver it. Their server is allowed to be down.** *A timeout is unknown. They may have processed it and failed to answer. The event id is what makes your retry safe.*\n\nSign the body. Retry 5xx and timeouts with backoff, then stop and surface the failure. A webhook that retries a 404 forever is an outage you cause. A slow endpoint must not hold a thread per pending event. A queue of deliveries is the design, the same shape as an outbox.\n\nLet them rotate the secret and see recent attempts. You will debug this on a call with them. You would not put the only copy of the event in the HTTP call."
  },
  "capacity-estimation": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Capacity estimation",
    "body": "**Start from users, not from servers**.\n\nOne million registered users, 100,000 of them active on a given day, 10 requests each. That is 1 million requests a day. A day has 86,400 seconds, so the average is about 12 requests per second. Peak is not the average. If the busy hour carries 10x, design the path for about 120 QPS. Write the assumption down so the interviewer can correct the 10x.\n\nStorage follows the same shape. 120 QPS with 2 KB stored per request is about 20 GB a day if every request is a write, which it is not. Count writes separately. A read-heavy shortener stores one row per create, not per redirect.\n\nBandwidth is QPS times response size. 120 QPS of 20 KB responses is about 2.4 MB/s, small for a NIC and not small for a phone path or a database you are pulling full rows from.\n\nCache size is the working set, not the whole database. If 5% of keys take 95% of reads and each value is 2 KB and there are a million hot keys, that is about 2 GB. It fits in Redis. The long tail does not need to.\n\nKafka throughput is events per second times bytes. 100,000 events/s at 1 KB is 100 MB/s of disk write, before replication multiplies it. Three replicas make that 300 MB/s of cluster disk. A 30 minute backlog is 180 GB of log. Retention and disk have to cover it.\n\nThe point of the number is the next sentence: the database can do 5k simple reads a second, the read path is 40k, so something else has to absorb the difference. That something is the design.\n\n**The number exists so the next sentence can name the bottleneck.** *Average is not peak, and a server count is not where you start.*\n\nIf the database does about 5,000 simple reads a second and the path is 40,000, the gap is the design: a cache, a log, or a shard. Say which box absorbs it. You would not open with a fleet size and hope the interviewer supplies the bottleneck."
  },
  "observability-pillars": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Logs, metrics, and traces",
    "body": "**Logs are what happened on one service, a line at a time**. You use them to see the error and the id. Structured logs, with fields rather than a sentence, are what you can search. A correlation id in every line is how you find one request across services.\n\nMetrics are numbers over time: request rate, error rate, latency, saturation. You alert on them. You do not alert on a log line if you can count it instead. A histogram of latency beats an average, because the average hides the slow tail.\n\nTraces follow one request through the services it touched. Each hop is a span. The trace id is the correlation id. OpenTelemetry is the library and the wire format you use so you are not locked to one vendor's agent. You don't need the collector topology. You need to say that logs, metrics, and traces share an id.\n\nIf you only have logs, you will grep during the incident and you will be late. If you only have averages, you will not see that 5% of requests take two seconds.\n\n**Logs tell you what one request did. Metrics tell you that the service is sick. Traces tell you which hop took the time.** *They share an id, or you cannot move from the alert to the request.*\n\nStructured fields beat a sentence. A histogram beats an average, because the average hides the tail. Alert on the metric, not on a log line you could have counted. OpenTelemetry is the library and the format so you are not locked to one vendor's agent. You do not need the collector diagram. You need the shared id.\n\nLogs alone mean you grep during the incident and you are late. Averages alone hide that 5% of requests take two seconds. You would not add three vendors and still forget the correlation id on the way out."
  },
  "sli-slo-sla": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "SLI, SLO, SLA, and error budgets",
    "body": "**An SLI is the measurement: the fraction of requests under 300 ms, or the fraction that returned success**. An SLO is the target you set internally: 99.9% of checkout requests succeed over 30 days. An SLA is the promise a customer can hold you to, usually with money attached. The SLO is tighter than the SLA so you notice first.\n\nThe error budget is what is left. A 99.9% SLO over 30 days allows about 40 minutes of total failure. If you have spent it by day 10, you stop shipping risky changes and you fix reliability. If you have plenty left, you can take a migration.\n\nPick SLIs a user would notice. CPU is not an SLI. Checkout success and latency are. Too many SLOs means none of them gate a release.\n\nThe budget is how you talk about reliability without saying \"zero downtime.\" Zero is not a number you can design to. 99.95% is, and it has a cost.\n\n**The SLI is what a user would notice. The SLO is your target. The SLA is what you owe, and it is looser so you notice first.** *CPU is not an SLI. Checkout success and latency are.*\n\nA 99.9% SLO over 30 days is about 40 minutes of failure. That remainder is the error budget. Spent by day 10, you stop the risky changes and fix reliability. Plenty left, you can take the migration. Too many SLOs means none of them gate a release.\n\nZero downtime is not a number you can design to. 99.95% is, and it has a cost. You would not set the SLO equal to the SLA. You would burn the customer promise before you had a signal."
  },
  "alerting": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Alerting",
    "body": "**Alert on symptoms a user feels, and on a few causes you know you must act on**. A page for \"error rate on checkout above 1% for 5 minutes\" is right. A page for \"CPU above 80%\" at 3 a.m. is how the team mutes the channel.\n\nRED is the service view: rate, errors, duration. USE is the machine view: utilization, saturation, errors. RED tells you the product is sick. USE tells you why a box is sick. Start with RED for paging. Look at USE while you are awake.\n\nA good alert has a next action. If the receiver cannot do anything, it is a dashboard. Lag on a password-reset consumer is a page. Lag on a weekly report is a ticket.\n\nBurn alerts from the SLO catch a slow bleed that no single threshold sees. A 10% error for five minutes and a 1% error for a day can both empty the budget. Threshold-only alerts miss the second one.\n\n**Page on a symptom the user feels, and only when the person on call can do something.** *CPU above 80% at 3 a.m. is how the channel gets muted. Checkout errors above 1% for five minutes is a page.*\n\nRED is rate, errors, and duration. That is the product. USE is utilization, saturation, and errors on the machine. Look at USE while you are awake. Lag on a password-reset consumer is a page. Lag on a weekly report is a ticket. If there is no next action, it is a dashboard.\n\nA burn alert catches a slow bleed that no single threshold sees. Ten percent for five minutes and one percent all day can both empty the budget. A threshold misses the second. You would not page on every log line that contains the word error."
  },
  "latency-debug": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Latency went from 100ms to 2s",
    "body": "**Start with when and how wide**. One endpoint or all of them. One region. Started at a deploy or at the top of the hour. The answer changes the next step.\n\nLook at the trace of a slow request, or at latency broken down by dependency if you don't have traces. The two seconds are usually one hop: a database query, a downstream timeout, a lock wait, a cold cache. If every hop is fast and the sum is slow, you are doing them in series and the count went up, which is an N+1.\n\nCheck the recent deploy, a cache hit ratio drop, a connection pool waiting, and garbage collection or CPU saturation. A pool that waits 1.8 seconds of the 2 seconds is the database or whatever is holding the connections, not the application code that runs after them.\n\nIf only p99 moved and the median did not, you have a tail: a lock, a slow tenant, a cold partition. If the median moved, the common path changed.\n\nMitigate before you fully understand if users are hurting. Shed the expensive feature, roll back the deploy, or fail over. Then keep the trace that shows the hop.\n\n**Find whether the median moved or only the tail, and whether it was one route or all of them.** *The two seconds are usually one hop: a query, a timeout, a lock, a cold cache, or an N+1 you added in series.*\n\nA trace, or latency split by dependency, shows the hop. If every hop is fast and the sum is slow, you are doing more of them. A pool that waits 1.8 of the 2 seconds is the dependency holding the connections, not the code after them. A deploy, a hit-ratio drop, and a GC pause are the other first checks. Tail only: a lock, a tenant, a cold partition. Median moved: the common path changed.\n\nIf users are hurting, roll back, shed the expensive feature, or fail over before the explanation is complete. Then keep the trace. You would not start by adding instances."
  },
  "authn-authz": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Authentication vs authorization",
    "body": "**Authentication is who is calling**. Authorization is what they may do. A valid token answers the first. It does not answer the second.\n\nThe gateway can authenticate: the signature is valid, the token is not expired, the issuer is yours. The service that owns the order authorizes: this subject may refund this order. If the gateway does both, it has to understand every resource, and it becomes the place every rule change waits.\n\n401 means you did not convince me who you are. 403 means I know who you are and the answer is no. Mixing them up makes clients refresh tokens when they should stop.\n\nService-to-service calls need an identity too. A network position (\"it came from inside the VPC\") is not an identity. A token or a client certificate is.\n\n**The gateway checks that the token is yours and unexpired. The service that owns the row checks that this subject may touch this row.** *A valid token is not permission. 401 means \"I do not know you.\" 403 means \"I know you, and no.\"*\n\nIf the gateway does both, it has to understand every resource, and every rule change waits there. Mixing 401 and 403 makes clients refresh a token when they should stop. A call from another service needs an identity too. \"It came from inside the VPC\" is not an identity. A token or a client certificate is.\n\nYou would not trust the user id in the body. You trust the subject in the token you verified, and then you load the row."
  },
  "sessions-vs-jwt": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Sessions vs JWT",
    "body": "**A session is a random id in a cookie**. The server, or Redis, stores the user and the expiry. Logging out deletes the row. The next request is anonymous immediately. The cost is a lookup on every request and a store you must keep up.\n\nA JWT is a signed set of claims. The server checks the signature and the expiry and does not call a store. Any service can verify with the public key. That is why it fits a fleet of services. The cost is revocation. A token is valid until `exp` even if the user logs out or you fire them. Keep access tokens short, minutes to an hour, and put a denylist in Redis only if you truly must revoke sooner.\n\n*Don't put secrets or a large permission matrix in the token*. It is base64, not encryption. Anyone who has it can read it. Put a subject id and a few roles. Look up the rest.\n\nPick sessions when you control one website and need instant logout. Pick JWT when many services must verify without sharing a session store, and you accept the expiry window.\n\n**A session can be deleted and the next request is anonymous. A JWT is valid until it expires, even after logout.** *Keep access tokens short. Do not put a secret or a large permission matrix in the token. It is signed, not encrypted. Anyone who has it can read it.*\n\nThe session id is random. Redis holds the user. Logout deletes the row. You pay a lookup, and you must keep the store up. The JWT is checked with the public key, so any service can verify without a shared store. That fits a fleet. Revocation is the cost. A denylist is only worth it when you must revoke before `exp`.\n\nPut a subject and a few roles. Look up the rest. Sessions fit one site that needs instant logout. JWT fits many services that can accept the expiry window."
  },
  "oauth-oidc": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect",
    "body": "**OAuth 2**.0 is delegated authorization. The user lets your app call an API without giving you their password. The result is an access token for that API, and often a refresh token.\n\nThe access token is short-lived and sent on each call. The refresh token is long-lived and is used only to get a new access token. Store the refresh token like a password. A stolen refresh token is a stolen session until it is revoked.\n\nThe useful grant for a server-side web app is authorization code with PKCE. The implicit grant that put tokens in the URL is obsolete. Client credentials are for a service calling another service with no user.\n\nOpenID Connect sits on OAuth and adds an id token about the user: who they are, not what they may do. OAuth alone does not have to tell you the user's name.\n\nValidate the issuer, audience, expiry, and signature. A token for another service, or signed by an issuer you don't trust, is not your user. Rotate signing keys with a JWKS endpoint rather than a key baked into the deploy.\n\n**Authorization code with PKCE for a user in a browser. Client credentials when a service calls a service and there is no user.** *The implicit grant that put tokens in the URL is obsolete. A refresh token is a long-lived secret. Store it like a password.*\n\nThe access token is what you send on each call, and it is short. The refresh token only gets a new access token. A stolen refresh token is a stolen session until you revoke it. OpenID Connect adds an id token about who the user is. OAuth alone does not have to tell you their name.\n\nValidate issuer, audience, expiry, and signature. A token for another API, or from an issuer you do not trust, is not your user. Rotate signing keys with a JWKS endpoint. You would not bake the key into the deploy and call that rotation."
  },
  "api-keys": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "API keys",
    "body": "**An API key identifies a program, not a person**. You issue it to a customer integration, hash it at rest, and show the secret once. A key in a log line is a leaked credential. Log a prefix or a hash.\n\nKeys are long-lived, so you need rotation and revocation. Two keys valid during a rotation beats a flag day. A key should be scoped: this partner can read orders, not refund them. A single key that can do everything is a password for the whole API.\n\nPrefer a key at the edge for quota and identity of the integration, and still authorize the action in the service. Don't build OAuth for a script that posts once a night if a key will do. Don't use a key for a browser app. It will end up in the JavaScript, and then it is public.\n\nRate-limit per key. One partner's bug should not spend the quota of the others.\n\n**A key identifies a program. Hash it at rest, show the secret once, and scope what it can do.** *A key in a log line is a leaked credential. Log a prefix or a hash. A key in browser JavaScript is public.*\n\nKeys live a long time, so you rotate by overlapping two keys instead of a flag day. \"This partner can read orders\" is a scope. A key that can do everything is a password for the whole API. Use it at the edge for quota and for who the integration is. Still authorize the action in the service. A nightly script does not need OAuth. A browser app does not get a key.\n\nRate-limit per key so one partner's bug does not spend everyone else's quota."
  },
  "mtls": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "mTLS",
    "body": "**Ordinary TLS proves the server to the client**. Mutual TLS also proves the client to the server, with a client certificate. Both sides have a key. There is no bearer token to steal out of a header and replay from a laptop, as long as the private key stays on the machine.\n\nYou use it between services, especially across a network you don't fully trust. A mesh often does the handshake in a sidecar so the app still sees plain HTTP locally. The certificate's identity becomes the service name you authorize on.\n\nThe cost is issuance and rotation. A platform that cannot mint short-lived certificates will end up with five-year certs and an outage when they expire. Automate that, or don't adopt mTLS.\n\nmTLS authenticates the service, not the user who triggered the call. You still want the user token if the action is on a user's behalf. The service identity says who is calling. The user token says who they are calling for.\n\n**Both sides present a certificate. There is no bearer token to copy out of a header and replay from a laptop, as long as the private key stays on the machine.** *mTLS authenticates the service, not the user who triggered the call. You still want the user token if the action is on a user's behalf.*\n\nA mesh often does the handshake in a sidecar so the app sees plain HTTP locally. The certificate's identity is the service name you authorize. The cost is issuance and rotation. A platform that cannot mint short-lived certificates ends up with five-year certs and an outage on the day they expire. Automate that, or do not adopt mTLS.\n\nYou would not replace the user check with \"the caller has a cert.\" That only proves which service is calling."
  },
  "password-hashing": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Password hashing",
    "body": "**A password is hashed with a slow algorithm and a unique salt, then the hash is stored**. You never store the password and you never encrypt it with a key you can decrypt, because a decryptable password is a password you will leak.\n\nUse bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. A single SHA-256 is fast, which is what an attacker wants. The salt stops a precomputed table from working across your users. The work factor is how slow the hash is. Raise it until a login costs tens of milliseconds, not until it costs a second and becomes a denial of service.\n\nA breach of the hash table is still serious. Slow hashes buy time to reset. They don't make a weak password strong. Length and a breach check matter more than a clever alphabet rule.\n\nStaff passwords and user passwords are the same problem. SSO so you don't hold a password at all is the better design for employees.\n\n**Hash with a slow algorithm and a unique salt. Never store the password, and never encrypt it with a key you could use to decrypt it.** *A single SHA-256 is what an attacker wants, because it is fast. bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 are the choices.*\n\nThe salt stops one precomputed table from working for every user. Raise the work factor until a login costs tens of milliseconds, not until it costs a second and becomes a denial of service. A stolen hash table is still serious. Slow hashes buy time to reset. They do not make a weak password strong.\n\nSSO so you do not hold an employee password at all is the better design for staff. You would not log the password \"just for debugging\" and you would not invent a reversible scheme so support can read it back."
  },
  "encryption": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Encryption at rest and in transit",
    "body": "**In transit means TLS on the wire**. Do it at the edge always, and between services whenever the network is shared. \"Inside the VPC\" is not an encryption story if other teams and vendors share that network.\n\nAt rest means the disk or the column is encrypted. Disk encryption protects a stolen drive and does almost nothing for an application bug or a stolen database password. Column or field encryption protects a specific value, such as a national id, from someone who can read the table but not the key.\n\nKeys do not live next to the data. A KMS holds them. The app asks to decrypt. You can revoke and you can audit. A key in the same repository as the ciphertext is not encryption. It is encoding.\n\nEncryption does not replace authorization. A service that is allowed to decrypt everything will decrypt everything when it is compromised. Scope the key to the data that service owns.\n\n**TLS on the wire, and a key that does not live next to the ciphertext.** *Disk encryption protects a stolen drive. It does almost nothing for a stolen database password or an application bug.*\n\nEncrypt at the edge always, and between services when the network is shared. \"Inside the VPC\" is not a story if other teams share that network. Field encryption protects a specific value, such as a national id, from someone who can read the table but not the key. A KMS holds the key. The app asks to decrypt. You can revoke and you can audit. A key in the same repository as the data is encoding.\n\nEncryption does not replace authorization. A service allowed to decrypt everything will, once it is compromised. Scope the key to the data that service owns."
  },
  "secrets": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "Secrets",
    "body": "**A secret is a password, a token, a private key, or a connection string**. It does not go in git, in an image layer, or in a log. An env var in a deploy system is better than a source file and worse than a secret store that injects it at runtime and rotates it.\n\nUse the cloud secret manager or Kubernetes secrets backed by a real store, with access limited to the workload identity. A developer should not need the production database password to deploy. The pod should.\n\nRotate. A secret you cannot rotate will live forever, and forever includes the laptop that copied it. Design the client to reload. A process that reads the password once at startup cannot rotate without a restart. Sometimes a restart is acceptable. Say so.\n\nWhen a secret leaks, you rotate and you assume it was used. Detection is an audit log of who read it, which a `.env` file does not have.\n\n**A password, a token, a private key, or a connection string does not go in git, in an image, or in a log.** *An environment variable in the deploy system is better than a source file and worse than a store that injects the value at runtime and can rotate it.*\n\nThe workload identity reads the secret. A developer should not need the production database password to deploy. The process should. A secret you cannot rotate lives forever, and forever includes the laptop that copied it. Design the client to reload, or accept that rotation is a restart, and say which.\n\nWhen it leaks, rotate and assume it was used. An audit of who read it is what a `.env` file does not have."
  },
  "rbac": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "RBAC and ABAC",
    "body": "**RBAC assigns permissions to roles, and roles to users**. An admin can refund. A support agent can read. You can explain it on a whiteboard, and you can audit it. It gets clumsy when the rule is \"refund only your region's orders under 100.\" You either explode the roles or you start special-casing.\n\nABAC uses attributes: the user's region, the order's amount, the time of day. It expresses that rule. It is harder to see why a decision happened, so you log the decision and the attributes.\n\nStart with RBAC. Add attributes for the few rules that are actually about the resource, not about the job title. \"Owner of this order\" is an attribute check in the service. It should not be a role.\n\nDo the check on the server that holds the resource. A role in a JWT can be stale or edited if you don't verify the signature, and even a valid role does not prove this user owns this id. Load the row and compare.\n\n**Roles for the job. An attribute check, on the service that holds the row, for \"this user owns this order.\"** *A role in a token does not prove they own this id. Load the row and compare.*\n\nAdmin can refund. Support can read. You can draw that and you can audit it. \"Refund only your region's orders under 100\" explodes the role list. That rule is attributes: region, amount, and the owner id. Log the decision and the attributes or you cannot explain a denial. Start with roles. Add attributes for the few rules that are about the resource.\n\nYou would not put \"owner of order 42\" in the token. The token would be stale the moment ownership changes, and the check would be in the wrong place."
  },
  "cors-csrf": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "CORS and CSRF",
    "body": "**CORS is a browser rule about which websites may read a response from your API**. It is not access control. A curl call ignores it. The browser sends a preflight for some requests, and your response says which origins, methods, and headers are allowed. Reflecting `*` with credentials is the misconfiguration. List the origins.\n\nCSRF is a different attack. The browser attaches the user's cookie to a request that another site triggered. The user is logged in, and the other site posts a transfer. CORS does not stop that, because the other site doesn't need to read the response.\n\nDefenses: a cookie that is `SameSite`, and a CSRF token that JavaScript on your origin can read and an attacker's page cannot. An `Authorization` header with a bearer token is not sent automatically, so classic CSRF does not apply. Cookie sessions need the defense.\n\n*Don't disable CORS globally to unblock a frontend*. Name the origin.\n\n**CORS decides which websites may read a response in a browser. It is not access control. curl ignores it.** *CSRF is a different attack: the browser attaches the user's cookie to a request another site triggered. CORS does not stop that, because the other site does not need to read the response.*\n\nList the origins. Reflecting `*` with credentials is the misconfiguration. Cookie sessions need `SameSite` and a token that your page can read and the attacker's page cannot. A bearer token in an `Authorization` header is not sent automatically, so classic CSRF does not apply.\n\nYou would not disable CORS globally to unblock a frontend. Name the origin. You would not treat a CORS header as proof the caller may refund."
  },
  "owasp": {
    "kind": "note",
    "title": "OWASP issues that come up",
    "body": "**Broken access control is the one that hurts**. The token is valid, and the id in the URL is someone else's order. Check ownership on every read and write. Guessable ids plus a missing check is the bug.\n\nInjection is a query or a command built by concatenating input. Use bound parameters. An ORM does not save you if you still concatenate a sort column. Allow-list the column name.\n\nSSRF is the server fetching a URL the user supplied. The user points it at your metadata service or your internal admin port. Resolve and allow-list hosts, and block link-local addresses.\n\nBroken authentication is weak session ids, tokens in logs, and missing expiry. Secrets in the client and verbose errors that return a stack or another user's row sit next to these.\n\nYou will not recite the whole OWASP list. You will say which of these the design prevents, and where the check lives.\n\n**The bug that hurts is a valid token and someone else's id in the URL.** *Check ownership on every read and write. Guessable ids plus a missing check is the incident.*\n\nInjection is a query or a command built by gluing input together. Bind the parameters. An allow-list is required for a sort column, because an ORM will not save a concatenated name. SSRF is the server fetching a URL the user supplied, aimed at your metadata service or an internal admin port. Allow-list hosts and block link-local addresses. Weak session ids, tokens in logs, and missing expiry sit next to these, as do secrets in the client and errors that return another user's row.\n\nYou will not recite the whole list. You will say which of these this design prevents, and where the check lives."
  }
};
